WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.980
Good afternoon.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.050
I'm Phillip Khoury.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.770
I'm Associate Provost at
MIT with responsibility

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:11.980
for the arts.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.790
And I want to welcome all of
you to this public presentation

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.170
by Robert Lepage, who is the
recipient of the 2012 Eugene

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.500
McDermott Award in
the Arts at MIT.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.420
We're very pleased
also to have with us

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.840
Peter Gelb, the general manager
of the Metropolitan Opera.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.900
We look forward to a
stimulating and lively dialogue

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.770
about technology and
stagecraft and storytelling.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.650
Before introducing our
distinguished guests,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.220
let me say a few words about
the McDermott Award, which

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.670
is one of the most generous arts
prizes in the United States.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.210
Established in 1974
by Margaret McDermott

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.060
to honor her late husband,
Eugene, co-founder of Texas

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.060
Instruments, the award
recognizes individuals

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.910
whose artistic trajectory
and body of work

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.730
indicate that they will
achieve the highest

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.960
distinction in their fields.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.099
The award is considered an
investment in the recipient's

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.260
future creative
work, rather than

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.330
a prize for a particular project
or a lifetime of achievement.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.250
Most of all, however
the McDermott Award

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.100
reflects MIT'S commitment
to risk-taking,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.250
problem solving, and
connecting creative minds

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.410
across disciplines.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:27.270
I want to especially thank
Mary McDermott Cook, daughter

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.710
of Margaret and
Eugene McDermott,

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.470
who is here this evening--

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.920
this afternoon, late evening--
in the front row here.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.940
She continues the great
commitment of her family

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.230
to the arts here at MIT
in their hometown, Dallas,

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.000
and in many, many other places.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.690
Mary, delighted you're here with
us to help honor Robert Lepage.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.022
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.500
Now, recounting just a few
aspects of Robert Lepage's work

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.240
will reveal why he is such an
ideal recipient of this award.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.780
Lepage's multidisciplinary
work defies categorization.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.840
As you will have learned from
the extraordinary array of work

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.150
in the timeline that was
projected as you entered

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.760
this auditorium, Mr.
Lepage is equally

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.070
talented as a playwright,
actor, film director,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.630
and stage director.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.910
He and his production
company, Ex Machina,

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.700
have toured numerous
productions internationally

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.910
to critical and popular acclaim,
from large multiple part

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.170
productions to one person plays,
including the Anderson Project,

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.660
recently presented in
Boston by Arts Emerson.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.240
He has created two Cirque
Du Soleil productions, KA,

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.700
a resident show at MGM
Grand in Las Vegas,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.330
and TOTEM, which will be
seen in Boston in June.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.840
He has acted in or directed
numerous feature films.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.410
He stage directed two musical
tours for rock star Peter

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.500
Gabriel, who I just learned
was here at our Media Lab

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.880
just yesterday.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.880
He has created spectacular
outdoor illuminations

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.550
and architectural
projections in Quebec Harbor,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.730
using the surface of the
Bunge grain elevators

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:08.960
as a giant screen.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.910
Spectacular, really.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.340
And you will understand
why Lepage's work resonates

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.370
with the creative values
of the MIT community.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.400
We at MIT particularly
admire his willingness

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.660
to cross disciplinary boundaries
in the pursuit of innovation.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.370
The collaborative
enterprise that

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.830
drives his creative output.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.160
His relentless experimentation
with new technologies in order

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.560
to reimagine for
the 21st century

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.110
the ritual origins of
multiple performance genres.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.620
Including, of course, opera,
which some people would say

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.860
is inherently the most
multidisciplinary of performers

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.900
for performance genre.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.100
Which brings me to our second
distinguished guest today--

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.510
Peter Gelb.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.940
Since becoming general manager
of the Metropolitan Opera

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.600
in 2006, Peter Gelb has
launched a host of initiatives

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.995
to revitalize the Met and to
connect it to a wider audience.

00:03:58.995 --> 00:04:00.370
Under his leadership,
the company

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.328
has recruited many of
the world's great theater

00:04:02.328 --> 00:04:05.062
directors, including
Robert Lepage.

00:04:05.062 --> 00:04:06.520
He also increased
the annual number

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.030
of new productions, secured more
performances from top singers,

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.630
and he launched the trailblazing
Peabody Award-winning series,

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.470
the Met Live in HD, which
presents live performance

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.870
transmissions in movie
theaters around the world.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:24.520
I'm interested to note that much
earlier in his career in 1978,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.670
Peter Gelb was an assistant
manager of the Boston Symphony

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:28.240
Orchestra.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.510
So, perhaps, one could say
that his illustrious career was

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.970
launched right here in Boston.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:37.570
In any case, he has won six
Emmy Awards and three Peabody

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.730
Awards since then.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.910
We owe special thanks to Mr.
Gelb for being here today,

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.670
since he must return to New York
immediately after this program

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.245
in order to make the curtain
for a performance tonight of Das

00:04:49.245 --> 00:04:53.410
Rheingold, the first opera
in Wagner's four part

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.300
Der Ring des Nibelungen.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.180
Directed by Robert Lepage,
the four operas in the cycle

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.130
have been presented over
the last two seasons,

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:01.547
and are being
presented this month

00:05:01.547 --> 00:05:06.280
and in May as a complete
cycle for the first time.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:10.360
Now Robert Lepage first began
directing opera in 1993,

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.850
and first collaborated
with the Metropolitan Opera

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.590
in 2008 and 2009 in that season,
when he directed La Damnation

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:18.400
de Faust--

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.340
The Damnation of Faust
by Hector Berlioz.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.950
Lepage's production used
an innovative combination

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.370
of virtual scenery,
interactive video projections,

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.860
and aerial acrobatics
to dramatize

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.030
the timeless melodramatic
plot of sin and redemption.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:37.000
His transformation of
Wagner's 16 hour Ring Cycle,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.610
however, is the most
ambitious production

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.370
the Met has ever attempted.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.830
The four works in the
cycle are Opera's most

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.620
challenging and
transcendent epic.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.630
The Met's new production,
which premiered

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:52.960
between September
2010 and January 2012,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.880
uses the groundbreaking
single set

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.640
comprised of 24
movable planks, which

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.890
together with
computerized projections

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:04.600
form every mythic scene and
action that Wagner imagined.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.880
We at MIT appreciate
the ambition

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.210
of this undertaking, especially
the scale of imagination

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.940
and the technical complexity
that the new production

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:14.080
entails.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.760
And we appreciate
the risk taking

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.270
involved in reconceptualizing
a revered masterpiece

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:21.370
in this way, including
the controversy

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.000
that such bold moves
can and will generate.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.450
Productive disruptions of
norms are healthy and necessary

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:31.090
for innovation, for they
generate new thinking

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.210
and inventions for
the benefit of us all.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:39.310
That's very Lepage,
and it's very MIT, too.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:41.380
We look forward now to
hearing from these two

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.210
great creative
minds about the Ring

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.120
and other aspects of Mr.
Lepage's diverse and expansive

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.590
body of work.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.300
Ladies and gentlemen,
please join me

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.549
in welcoming Robert
Lepage and Peter Gelb.

00:06:52.549 --> 00:06:55.972
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.612
Not used to sitting
in such a deep--

00:07:15.612 --> 00:07:16.525
[LAUGHS]

00:07:16.525 --> 00:07:18.650
I don't know what controversy
you're talking about,

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:24.500
but I'm Peter Gelb,
this is Robert Lepage,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.630
as I'm sure you know.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:30.890
I am very happy to be here, to
have this conversation in front

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.620
of you with Robert.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.230
It's true that my career
did begin in Boston,

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.600
but I haven't been here
much in recent years.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:44.540
I mostly stayed in contact by
watching Yankee Red Sox games.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:47.870
But I won't go any further.

00:07:52.203 --> 00:07:53.120
I don't like gloating.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.090
The Red Sox win.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:01.080
They win their share.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.260
When I was appointed to
the Met at the end of 2004,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.680
I was determined to
reconnect the Met

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.650
to the cultural
mainstream, which

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:14.280
is not necessarily an easy thing
to do with an opera company

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.550
with 128 or
129-year-old history.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.210
And that was in recent decades
not used to making much

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.920
in the way of change.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.310
But I was determined
that we had to change

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.640
the very essence of what
we put on the stage,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:33.000
and the way in which we
connected with our audiences,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.370
both inside the opera house
and outside the opera house.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:43.200
And I sought the help of a
number of leading directors.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.750
But the person whose
help I sought, I think,

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.690
first, was Robert Lepage.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:52.260
Because I had known his
work and I've known him

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.460
for about 20 years,
and I believed

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:01.830
that the way to change
the basic fabric of opera

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:07.020
required bringing in
great creative minds

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.370
and visionary people
with visionary ideas,

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:12.930
such as Robert.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.090
And in fact, there's no
director in the world

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:19.710
that I know of
today who deserves,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.290
I think, this prestigious
award more than Robert.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:27.030
His visual imagination
and knack at storytelling

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.480
is what sets him apart from--

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.150
or, not to say there aren't
other great directors who

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.580
have great visual imagination
and are great storytellers.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:41.910
But Robert's has
an individual voice

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:47.790
which is remarkable in that it
is also, no two projects of his

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:52.313
seem to bear any direct visual
connection to each other.

00:09:52.313 --> 00:09:54.480
Each thing he does-- which
is very unusual, I think,

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.910
for directors-- each project
that he takes on really

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:03.130
has a soul and a life and
artistic character of its own.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:06.090
So because of that, and
because of my knowledge

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.580
of Robert's career, I
sort of instinctively

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.710
understood that it
was he who I had

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:14.250
to go to direct the
Met's new Ring Cycle.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:19.260
Which is, because of its scale
and sheer length of 16 hours

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.400
long, four operas,
is the single--

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.560
even if you don't
approach it on the scale

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.710
that Robert approached it on--

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:32.820
it's the largest undertaking
any opera company could possibly

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:35.400
take on.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:40.680
And since I believe in
taking risks in order

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.230
to change the face of
opera and advance it,

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:47.460
I embraced and supported
all of Robert's

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:50.790
revolutionary technological
ideas for this ring,

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:57.000
even though sometimes it seemed
that my fate and the fortunes

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.770
of the Met were
swinging in the balance.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:01.960
[CHUCKLES]

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:02.460
But.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:10.020
But for a new Ring, I would
rather gamble with Robert

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.700
than with anybody else.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:16.650
And in a few hours,
as Philip mentioned,

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.140
a few hours from now,
we're beginning the first

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.500
really tightly
scheduled Ring Cycle

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:24.390
of this new production,
which is why

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.600
I have to fly back to New York.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.850
Robert wisely staying behind.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.490
But his work has been done.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:41.550
Last night, in fact, timed with
the opening of our new Ring

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:46.020
Cycles was the premiere of
a fascinating documentary

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:50.730
film about the making of this
Ring, in which documentary

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:55.320
filmmaker Susan Froemke spent,
I guess, about five years

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.180
following Robert and his team.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:00.450
And because of my long
relationship with her

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.427
and trust in her, I gave
sort of complete access

00:12:03.427 --> 00:12:05.010
to everything that
went on at the Met.

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.050
Almost complete access.

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:10.830
And the film premiered at
the Tribeca Film Festival

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.670
last night, and will be shown
in movie theaters later on.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.750
In fact, next month.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.500
But I thought it would
be interesting just

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:20.610
to get things rolling,
we have a lot of--

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.920
Robert has brought
a lot of video,

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.650
and his colleagues
have arranged for us

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:28.140
to see a lot of interesting
examples of his work.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:32.160
But I thought just to give
you a sense of the scale

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.250
and scope of this new
Ring, a taste of it,

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.580
we would play this
trailer for the movie,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:41.730
for this documentary, which
we're going to do so now.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:45.420
If we could play the Wagner
Dream trailer, please.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:49.300
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:52.960
- Nothing defines an opera
house more than new productions.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:55.960
Opera has to be constantly
renewing itself.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:58.540
The Metropolitan Opera
kicks off its new season

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:02.050
with a lavish production of
Wagner's four-part Ring Cycle.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.670
It's the most
ambitious production

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:05.560
the Met has ever mounted.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.870
- This is as big as it
gets for an opera company.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:09.940
- No new production
is more significant.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.300
- The story of the Ring is the
creation and the destruction

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:14.020
of the world.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:15.550
16 hours of opera.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:19.240
This is an intense
challenge for any director.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.290
- Robert Lepage.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:22.220
One of theater's
great visionaries.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:26.260
- We use state of the art tools
to present a really fresh, new,

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:27.940
avant-garde crazy ring.

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:30.790
- I went to Iceland, digging
into the Icelandic roots.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.970
Tectonic sets, breaking
apart and separating,

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:35.860
suits the Scandinavian myths.

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:36.750
- I love Wagner.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.530
I'm afraid of what they're
going to do with it, though.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.400
- Parts of it have
always been unstagable.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:43.330
Swimming underwater, walking
over rainbow bridges,

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.010
riding on winged horses.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:47.770
- Nobody has gotten
it perfectly right.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:49.807
- That's what
we're trying to do.

00:13:49.807 --> 00:13:51.640
- Deborah Voigt, you'll
be singing Brunhilde

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.000
for the very first time.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.000
- There were only one or
two or three Brunhildes

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.542
who can command the
stage at the Met.

00:13:56.542 --> 00:13:59.320
- There are so many
challenging vocal passages.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:01.780
It can be defining
in her career.

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:03.312
- It was so daunting
for such a time

00:14:03.312 --> 00:14:05.020
that I thought, are
you out of your mind?

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.280
It's a very emotional
journey, this one.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:08.440
- Have you seen those sets?

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:11.362
- I'm a little bit nervous
about standing on it.

00:14:11.362 --> 00:14:12.570
[MACHINERY CLICKING]

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:13.522
- Stop it.

00:14:13.522 --> 00:14:14.230
- Emergency stop!

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:15.070
- You were never in danger.

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:15.670
Everything was locked in.

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:17.830
- I don't want to lose
my feet or whatever.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:19.990
- This is when the
real pros come out.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:20.830
- You scared?

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:22.150
- I'm so scared, I'm sorry.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:23.050
- Everything OK?

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.820
- So far, so good.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.320
- I was compared
to being, you know,

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:27.612
kind of a Christopher Columbus.

00:14:27.612 --> 00:14:29.230
You have to convince
a whole crew

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.060
that you're not going to fall
off the edge of the Earth,

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:33.643
that you are going to discover
something, you know it's there,

00:14:33.643 --> 00:14:34.750
and you'll get there.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:38.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.270
- If this is what
opera is like--

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.331
- We want more.

00:14:59.331 --> 00:15:02.415
[END PLAYBACK]

00:15:02.415 --> 00:15:03.227
It's beautiful.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:12.895
So that's just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:15:12.895 --> 00:15:15.640
You can say that again.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.890
So, Robert, why don't you--

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:22.570
I know that you believe
that storytelling

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:25.855
is more important than anything
else in anything you stage.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:32.570
And technology obviously played
a huge part in this production.

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:34.940
But maybe you can
talk a little bit

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:39.500
about how you harness
the technology,

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.390
and why you harnessed
the technology that you

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:41.990
did harness.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:45.500
And the story of how
you came to this.

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:46.130
Yeah.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:49.280
Well, actually, I've
always been an artist

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:52.130
who had problems really
defining himself.

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:55.580
You know, I'm a stage director
but I'm also an actor,

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:57.365
I'm also a filmmaker.

00:15:57.365 --> 00:15:59.090
Or I'm all of the
above, whatever.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:01.340
But it's not important exactly
what I do for a living.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.520
The idea is that I like mingling
with different people who

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:05.820
work in different disciplines.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:08.720
And I think that the
opera, certainly-- well,

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.580
the stage in general--
but let's say

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:15.230
the opera more than theater,
more than dance, is a place,

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:17.090
is a great meeting point.

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:21.710
And Wagner spoke of
the gesamtkunstwerk.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.320
He dreamed of the
gesamtkunstwerk, which means

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.890
the together artistic work.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:29.390
Which is a literal
translation that

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:32.180
means the meeting
point, the gathering

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.360
of all sorts of people
from different disciplines.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:42.830
And I really believe that opera
is the great multidisciplinary

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:45.140
way of working, way
of telling a story.

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:46.940
So of course, it's
always a question

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:48.980
of finding the
balance between who

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.220
tells what part of the
story, and what's redundant,

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:56.600
and what's overstated.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:01.970
And so for me, the whole
technological research

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:03.800
or presence in my
work is just there

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:07.849
as a tool to merge these
disciplines together.

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:10.849
I work in a field, well,
I'm mainly a stage director

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:11.630
from the theater.

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:14.450
So that means I work a
lot in the live world.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.270
But I'm also interested
by the recorded world.

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:19.369
I like doing films
once in a while,

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:22.130
and I like doing
television stuff.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:25.310
And I think that in
the 21st century,

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:29.270
audiences are going to want
something more of theater

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:30.950
and something more of film.

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:35.960
And the qualities of theater
are being more and more asked

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.500
from the recorded medium,
which has become a kind of,

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.770
of course you have the
intimacy in movies because

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:45.380
of the close ups and all
that, and the great sound.

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:50.068
But you don't have the liveness,
the interaction, with film

00:17:50.068 --> 00:17:51.110
that you have in theater.

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:52.130
And then vice versa.

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:54.050
With the theater
world, you don't

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:59.000
have the sense of intimacy if
you're in a 2000 seat room.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.420
You're not as
close to the actor.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:03.710
So how do you get all of
the advantages of film?

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:06.110
So I think that the way
for these two worlds

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:09.330
that are very, very close
cousins, because we're

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.330
dealing with actors, we're
dealing with writers,

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:13.250
with storytellers,
and we're trying

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:16.370
to convey the same
ideas, the same emotions.

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:19.280
I think that the more the
technology is the same,

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:22.580
that the more that the
lighting you use in film

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:25.460
and the lighting
that I use on stage

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:29.960
is the same
instruments, or the data

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:33.890
or the codes or any
kind of technology that

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:37.200
is being used in both
worlds is common,

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:38.248
then there's a dialogue.

00:18:38.248 --> 00:18:39.290
Then there's an openness.

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:43.700
And then I believe that the
21st century will probably

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:47.450
be the scene of some
eighth form of art,

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:51.140
which will be a great
meeting point of all

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:52.170
of those disciplines.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:56.030
And, of course,
this is one attempt.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:59.360
The opera world is a
great place to try that.

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.660
It's full of traps
and obstacles,

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:07.850
but as we say in Buddhism, your
obstacles are your friends.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:12.500
So, of course, I've made a lot
of friends on this experience.

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:14.390
But I believe in that, really.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:15.470
I really believe in that.

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:17.150
I still believe in
the stage, I still

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:21.800
believe in people
gathering in the same room,

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.930
seeing artists taking
risks, because it's

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:25.190
a very, very risky thing.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:28.070
And certainly opera is a sport.

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:31.100
And actually what I find
remarkable in your way

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:34.310
of presenting it in
the HD transmissions,

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:39.650
it is like an exciting hockey
game or an exciting football

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:43.490
game because you have
the singers concentrating

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:48.650
and stepping on stage,
singing their heart out.

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:50.930
And then as soon as they
walk off, you catch them

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:52.192
and you say, so how was it?

00:19:52.192 --> 00:19:53.150
You know, and all that.

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:56.300
So it's a bit crazy,
but for some reason,

00:19:56.300 --> 00:20:00.860
opera lends itself to that,
because it's a larger than life

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:01.520
effort.

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:04.340
It's a larger than life
way of telling a story.

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:07.730
And I think it's, I
really truly still believe

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:09.960
in opera and its potential.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:15.770
It's just been ignored a bit,
or it hasn't been as accessible,

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:19.312
let's say, to everybody
in the past century

00:20:19.312 --> 00:20:20.270
as it should have been.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:23.630
But it used to be in the 19th
century much more accessible

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:26.740
and much more popular.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:28.490
Well, obviously, I
mean, some the problems

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:29.600
are the length of operas--

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:30.410
Also.

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:35.830
--and the languages that you
have to read subtitles for.

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:39.320
It's sort of like going to
a foreign film or something.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:41.360
You have to be able to over--

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:42.560
be willing to accept.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:43.430
Absolutely, yeah.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:44.300
There's a lot of conventions.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:46.217
But that's the thing
that I find extraordinary

00:20:46.217 --> 00:20:51.290
about the [? HG ?] transmissions
from my hometown, Quebec City,

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:54.020
the rooms are filled with people
who've never been to the opera

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:54.520
before.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:58.760
You see grandfathers and their
children explaining the story.

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.980
Of course, it's in German
with English subtitles,

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.480
and they're French
speaking people.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:03.320
I mean, of course.

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.870
But there's a passion,
there's a mystery there.

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:10.550
There's something that
seems to attract them.

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:14.120
So I think there's a
lot of hope in this.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.990
Getting to the essence of the
Ring, when you, since this is

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:22.760
probably the single
biggest project

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:28.400
I guess you've undertaken, as
well as I have, at the Met.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:30.890
There has been some,
as Philip mentioned,

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:34.070
some controversy
around it, which

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:39.350
seems to be mostly
out of your choice to,

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:42.950
even though you're using the
most modern technological means

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:47.060
to tell the story
that Wagner wrote.

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.550
As opposed to doing it in
some sort of more abstract,

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:50.210
metaphorical kind of fashion.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:50.900
Yeah.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:53.317
Well there's something, I mean,
very few people know this.

00:21:53.317 --> 00:21:58.880
Is that I hope Wagner fans
or opera fans will eventually

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:01.400
have the chance to
read the whole score

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.470
and the whole
libretto of the Ring.

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:05.940
But it reads like a film script.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:06.440
And--

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:07.575
Do you want to just--

00:22:07.575 --> 00:22:09.950
I mean, I presume most people
know the story of the Ring,

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:10.950
but you want to give a--

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.700
[LAUGHING] You really
want me to put myself

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:15.020
through that exercise?

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:16.280
But--

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:18.500
We can tell it together.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.000
No, but the thing
is, the reason why

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:21.417
I say it reads
like a film script,

00:22:21.417 --> 00:22:27.050
is because what Wagner asks of
the stage designer and lighting

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:31.610
designer and the director,
what he asks of us is a movie.

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:33.260
And at a time where film--

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.960
And in fact, when Wagner
is, as was pointed out

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:40.010
in this documentary, when
Wagner created the Ring,

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:42.620
he built this theater to
put it on in Bayreuth,

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:45.510
and then produced it, but was
very unhappy with the results.

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:46.010
Of course.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:46.520
Because he didn't have--

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:47.750
He couldn't, because
he had n-- he didn't

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.000
have the technological,
the technical means,

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.250
to represent the story,
this impossible story

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:53.840
that he had composed.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:58.220
And it just means that he
was a Freudian before Freud.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:00.080
He was cinematic before cinema.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:03.560
So, you know, that's the
great genius of Wagner.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:04.553
And so--

00:23:04.553 --> 00:23:06.845
That's one of the reasons
why you decided to actually--

00:23:06.845 --> 00:23:07.345
Absolutely.

00:23:07.345 --> 00:23:10.810
And to put myself at the
service of these demands.

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:12.650
So, of course, there's
a lot of people

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:15.410
who think that there's no
personal vision of the Ring.

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:21.500
But for me, it's my entrance
into the Wagnerian world.

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:25.340
I have in front of me a monument
that's much bigger than myself,

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:27.730
and I want to learn from it.

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:31.730
So I tried to be as faithful
to the story as possible,

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:33.020
and in every detail.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:37.340
And see how in this day and age,
if we do have the technology

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:40.490
to accomplish many,
many miracles,

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:43.430
how can we actually
illustrate these things?

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:46.730
And sometimes people have been
very critical of some elements

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:50.300
that I've staged that were
actually demands from Wagner.

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:52.300
Well, I think the
public loves it.

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:54.500
And the proof of that
is in the reaction

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:55.730
inside the opera house.

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:59.540
You know, we have some video
examples of some of the things

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:04.400
that you did, which
Wagner, as you said,

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:08.270
demanded or asked
for various actions

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:10.700
to be depicted that
opera houses, when

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:12.080
they produce the Ring never do.

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:14.420
Because they don't have the
scenery or the wherewithal

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.430
to do it.

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:21.590
So you, with this machine that
you and your designers created,

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:24.380
invented, set out
from the beginning

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:29.270
to be able to realize all these
scenic ideas of Wagner that

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:32.773
typically are heard musically,
but never actually represented

00:24:32.773 --> 00:24:33.315
on the stage.

00:24:33.315 --> 00:24:33.920
Exactly.

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:35.920
So do you want, is there
one you wanted to show?

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:37.760
Well, maybe, one of
the first excerpts

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:40.750
is maybe the descent
down into Valhalla.

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:41.250
Nibelheim.

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:42.380
Oh, no, sorry.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:44.000
To Nibelheim.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:47.330
Actually, there is a
huge chunk of music

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:51.800
where there's a suggestion
by Wagner where we actually

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:52.910
see Wotan.

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:57.050
So this, just to set the stage,
so Wotan, the King of the gods,

00:24:57.050 --> 00:24:59.810
has reneged on his
deal with the giants

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:02.060
who built his new castle.

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:06.770
And he realizes, though, that
he's in a lot of trouble.

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:11.480
And to get out of it,
he and his aide de camp,

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:15.950
Loge, the God of fire, decide
to go down into the underworld

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:23.360
where Nibelheim, where Alberich,
the dwarf who was previously,

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:25.880
in the beginning of the
opera, has stolen the gold

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:29.370
from the Rhine, which sets off
this whole cataclysmic chain

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.500
of events.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:35.640
Alberich is down in Nibelheim
with his fellow dwarves

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:38.940
making more treasure,
and planning

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:43.410
for an assault on the
gods and the powers above.

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:46.120
So Wotan goes down
there with Loge.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:48.840
Normally this is not seen.

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.898
A curtain comes in and you
hear the music, and the next--

00:25:51.898 --> 00:25:53.190
And you're supposed to imagine.

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:54.480
You're supposed to imagine it.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:55.860
Of course, if you
know the libretto

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:56.950
and if you know the story.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:58.533
So maybe you could
look at an excerpt.

00:25:58.533 --> 00:26:00.288
Of course, we'll
be doing this a bit

00:26:00.288 --> 00:26:02.580
accelerated, because the
excerpts can be a bit longish.

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.530
But we might speed
it up on the way

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:06.390
so you can get to
appreciate all of it.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:09.677
Although this one might
wreak havoc with the museum.

00:26:09.677 --> 00:26:13.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:38.402
So, in fact--

00:28:38.402 --> 00:28:44.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:45.990
They eventually get there.

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:48.480
Yeah, they eventually get there.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:51.250
Actually, maybe to give
you a sense of the scope,

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:54.480
we could watch another excerpt,
which is the excerpt at the end

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:58.920
of the Walküre, when Wotan
puts his daughter Brunhilde

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.000
to sleep.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.830
It gives you maybe
a better scope

00:29:01.830 --> 00:29:03.480
of what the whole project was.

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:05.340
And we should
point out that this

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:08.060
is one of the, maybe the
first opera, certainly

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:09.810
the first opera in the
history of the Met,

00:29:09.810 --> 00:29:12.630
to utilize stunt doubles.

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:13.425
Also.

00:29:13.425 --> 00:29:16.050
But some of the stunts have been
actually performed, sometimes,

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:17.800
by some of the performers.

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:19.212
Those were two acrobats.

00:29:19.212 --> 00:29:19.920
Yeah, absolutely.

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:20.420
But--

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:22.590
We don't normally
send our singers--

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:24.423
But it happened at times
that some singers--

00:29:24.423 --> 00:29:25.882
Unless you want to
get rid of them.

00:29:25.882 --> 00:29:28.530
--some singers would show up
and look at the stunt and say,

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:29.875
I can do that.

00:29:29.875 --> 00:29:31.500
And say, well, I'm
not sure you should.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.020
But actually, we
actually got to do it.

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:35.280
So maybe we could look at
maybe another excerpt right

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:39.228
away just to give you a sense
of the scope of the, um.

00:29:39.228 --> 00:29:42.714
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:25.460 --> 00:31:28.460
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:36.010
So what you've seen basically
is the end of Walküre--

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:37.180
The second opera.

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:38.050
The second opera.

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:41.650
And so what was very
challenging for us

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:45.670
is to try to create a
stage that has something

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:47.680
very traditional about
it, because it's actually

00:31:47.680 --> 00:31:48.940
24 planks.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:51.850
And when it starts,
it's in a rake position,

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:57.670
so it looks like a traditional
opera house raked floor.

00:31:57.670 --> 00:31:59.282
And it all looks
very, very normal.

00:31:59.282 --> 00:32:00.740
But then eventually,
when it starts

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:05.918
with the beginning of the
Rheingold and we try to--

00:32:05.918 --> 00:32:07.460
normally it's the
music of the Rhine,

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:12.040
so we, the plank starts
rocking, and eventually become

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:13.930
very, very sensuous
water, and eventually

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:16.600
evolve into all sorts
of different locations.

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:20.075
And, of course, as the
story evolves, it becomes,

00:32:20.075 --> 00:32:21.700
it illustrates many,
many, many things.

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:26.680
So what it was
inspired by, basically,

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:33.430
was the idea that some of
those-- well, most of the Ring

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:36.640
comes from the Icelandic Eddas.

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:40.030
And the Eddas are
pretty much like the,

00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:44.300
kind of a Bible in Icelandic
and Viking culture.

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:47.320
So Wagner, we keep forgetting
that Wagner actually

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:50.110
borrowed those heroes
and these stories

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:52.240
to create the story of the Ring.

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:57.460
And because of that, and I've
been to Iceland a few times.

00:32:57.460 --> 00:33:02.980
And I was struck by how,
because in a past life,

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:05.440
I was interested in
geography and geology.

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:07.780
And I was all into
tectonic plates

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:10.780
and the movement of continents
and volcanic activity,

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:11.380
and all that.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:12.400
You did your show.

00:33:12.400 --> 00:33:14.170
I even did a show
called Tectonic Plates.

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:16.753
And Iceland is, I don't know if
you've been to Iceland before,

00:33:16.753 --> 00:33:21.790
but it's a tiny island
of a country that's

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:23.320
sitting in the
middle of the ridge

00:33:23.320 --> 00:33:25.060
where actually, the world is--

00:33:25.060 --> 00:33:29.170
America and Europe are
kind of separating.

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:31.600
And even the
country's separating.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:34.750
Each year there's a
fault, and that opens

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:36.790
six centimeters each year.

00:33:36.790 --> 00:33:42.880
So it's a moving place, and
the ground is a moving--

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:44.380
it's not a stable place.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:46.018
And it has all
sorts of eruptions,

00:33:46.018 --> 00:33:47.560
and all sorts of
states and all that.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:51.400
And when you visit Iceland,
you have a very different take

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:51.940
on the Ring.

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:54.310
Because you go, oh, I
can understand somebody,

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:59.860
a culture living here and
imagining how all elements

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:01.030
manifest themselves.

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:03.400
And how they do believe
that there is a god of fire

00:34:03.400 --> 00:34:06.440
and a god of thunder and a
god of this and a god of that.

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:10.120
So we wanted to create
something that would

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:11.420
be tectonic in a certain way.

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:14.500
There was some kind
of, where the ground

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:16.330
wouldn't be very
stable, which was very

00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:17.694
alarming for a lot of singers.

00:34:17.694 --> 00:34:20.290
[LAUGHS] Because they were,
oh, you're walking on something

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:21.699
that moves all the time.

00:34:21.699 --> 00:34:25.389
The singer we just saw,
the end of Walküre,

00:34:25.389 --> 00:34:28.690
was the singer Byrn
Terfel, who's Wotan,

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:32.320
the ruler of the gods
on the foreground.

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:37.179
And he was just sentenced
his daughter Brunhilde

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:42.810
for disobeying him to endless
sleep surrounded by fire.

00:34:42.810 --> 00:34:43.690
On top of mountain.

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:44.648
On top of the mountain.

00:34:44.648 --> 00:34:48.670
But that actual singer who
plays Brunhilde had exited,

00:34:48.670 --> 00:34:52.120
and this was her stunt double.

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:54.219
Body double, yeah.

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:57.810
Sort of the operatic
equivalent of the piano drop.

00:34:57.810 --> 00:35:03.280
[LAUGHING] The soprano drop.

00:35:08.350 --> 00:35:09.580
So.

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:10.270
Yeah.

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:12.520
What else should we
talk about the Ring?

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:16.540
Do you want to talk about
a bit about the creation

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:18.700
of the design of the set?

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:21.130
Or do you have some animation
that could be, maybe--

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:23.610
Yeah, we have, just so
you understand a bit how,

00:35:23.610 --> 00:35:24.850
the premise of all this.

00:35:24.850 --> 00:35:30.460
We have a clip of the set
designer, Carl Fillion,

00:35:30.460 --> 00:35:33.010
who's a set designer who is
actually an amazing stage

00:35:33.010 --> 00:35:33.700
engineer.

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:36.790
I've been working with him
in Cirque Du Soleil a lot,

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:39.292
and doing some other
theatrical creations, actually.

00:35:39.292 --> 00:35:40.750
But you work,
actually, with many--

00:35:40.750 --> 00:35:42.625
we're going to get to
that in a little while.

00:35:42.625 --> 00:35:44.830
But you're, unlike--
most directors

00:35:44.830 --> 00:35:46.765
have one team they work with.

00:35:46.765 --> 00:35:48.890
You work with many teams,
many different directors.

00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:50.265
I mean, many
different designers.

00:35:50.265 --> 00:35:51.010
Yes, absolutely.

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:51.510
Yeah.

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:54.550
Of course, Carl probably
was, for a long time,

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:55.300
the principal one.

00:35:55.300 --> 00:35:57.325
Because I was asked
to do, I'm always

00:35:57.325 --> 00:35:59.450
interested to do these big,
big shows and all that.

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:03.070
And Carl has, his
back is strong enough

00:36:03.070 --> 00:36:05.150
to actually take
on these projects.

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:08.738
So maybe we could show some of
his 3D animations of the sets

00:36:08.738 --> 00:36:10.030
so you'd understand a bit more.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:11.740
And that's something we
could speed up, right?

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:12.700
Yeah, this we could speed up.

00:36:12.700 --> 00:36:13.200
Yeah.

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:15.340
And it's actually-- so
this is the princess.

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:17.890
When we were saying
that it starts with the,

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:20.890
this is the beginning where
the Rhine maidens come out,

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:23.140
and of course, they're
suspended by the set.

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:26.070
So this was created
in 2007 or 2008?

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:26.570
Yeah, yeah.

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:27.920
Yeah, absolutely.

00:36:27.920 --> 00:36:31.160
So this is kind of
a sped up version.

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:31.690
So--

00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:33.640
Those are the Rhine maidens
frolicking in the Rhine.

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:34.140
Mm-hmm.

00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:38.230
And then eventually,
they come across--

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:40.780
I think in this version, yeah.

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:44.950
So that becomes a
beach, and then they

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:46.360
comes out with the Rhine.

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:48.160
The Rheingold in those
days was supposed

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:50.870
to be a big golden cloth,
but that all changed.

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:53.020
And then of course,
they're being brought back

00:36:53.020 --> 00:36:55.630
into the depths of the Rhine.

00:36:55.630 --> 00:37:01.360
And then we had, in those
days, we had Wotan down

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:05.110
and Fricka sleeping.

00:37:05.110 --> 00:37:08.980
And, I guess-- so
it shows you a bit

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:11.950
how we played around
with the actual set.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:14.380
This is, those are
the giants visiting.

00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:17.860
Because we kind of tried
to envision the Giants

00:37:17.860 --> 00:37:20.110
as two giant hands coming
to them and saying,

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:21.560
we want to be paid.

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:25.210
So we created these giant hands.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:29.680
And the hands would actually
come and kidnap Freia.

00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:36.220
And then after that, they
all decided to go down to,

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:38.650
that's one of the excerpts
that we saw earlier on, when

00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:40.060
it becomes a giant staircase.

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:42.820
And decide to go
down to Nibelheim.

00:37:48.760 --> 00:37:51.525
[LAUGHING] So, of
course, you have

00:37:51.525 --> 00:37:52.900
to understand that
in those days,

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:55.442
we didn't have the vaguest idea
how we were going to do this.

00:37:55.442 --> 00:37:59.770
[LAUGHING] We just
thought it would be

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:02.840
an interesting way of doing it.

00:38:02.840 --> 00:38:07.613
So then, of course, the set
became the body of the dragon.

00:38:07.613 --> 00:38:09.280
And it's interesting
to see it this way,

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:10.322
because it's quite naked.

00:38:10.322 --> 00:38:12.340
Because all of this
is, there's a lot

00:38:12.340 --> 00:38:16.720
of interactive
projections on this set.

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:20.870
But this was like the bare
bones of the whole piece.

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:24.265
So, I don't know if we have
the equivalent for Walküre

00:38:24.265 --> 00:38:25.460
or for the others.

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:26.873
But I think that's--

00:38:26.873 --> 00:38:27.790
That's pretty much it.

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:30.280
--what we've got, yeah.

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:34.264
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:35.613 --> 00:38:37.280
Are there any other
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:38:37.280 --> 00:38:37.780
of the Ring?

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:42.620
Well, maybe, just to maybe
give you an example of--

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:45.138
people think I'm obsessed by
big machines and technology

00:38:45.138 --> 00:38:45.680
and all that.

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:47.630
I always end up working
with these things,

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:50.510
but I'm interested
in low tech, also.

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:54.410
And in the very beginning
of how you use technology.

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:56.145
And I did this
other opera that's

00:38:56.145 --> 00:38:58.520
performing right now, who was
performing, because they're

00:38:58.520 --> 00:38:59.930
on strike in Lyon.

00:38:59.930 --> 00:39:04.880
But this week, I had the
Nightingale, a Stravinsky piece

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:06.270
that I've been working on.

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:11.690
And we went to Vietnam to meet
with some Vietnamese water

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:12.710
puppet masters.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:15.740
And the whole
adventure that time

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:20.120
was to convince opera houses
to empty the orchestra pit,

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:22.940
to put the orchestra on
stage, to fill the orchestra

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:24.780
pit with water.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:26.990
We have not done
that at the Met.

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:27.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:33.470
And the actual singers
are waist deep in water

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:36.650
and manipulate Vietnamese
water puppets and sing.

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:39.890
So maybe we have some
excerpts of that.

00:39:39.890 --> 00:39:42.550
We could show you some of the
stuff that we've managed to do.

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:44.450
And that's in Lyon right
now, and it's been--

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:50.930
so in this case,
there's no projections,

00:39:50.930 --> 00:39:52.310
there's no engineering.

00:39:52.310 --> 00:39:54.770
It's good old puppetry.

00:39:54.770 --> 00:39:57.110
And so we have bunraku
puppetry involved.

00:39:57.110 --> 00:39:59.120
And this is actually a singer.

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:01.760
And the chorus in
the back all had

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:07.170
to learn how to manipulate
Taiwanese style puppets.

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:07.670
And--

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:09.190
It's really fantastic.

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:14.660
So this is one of the
excerpts of the piece.

00:40:14.660 --> 00:40:18.980
We also, in the first
part of that opera,

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:22.590
we also worked with very,
very, very low tech.

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:24.020
With a lot of shadowplay.

00:40:24.020 --> 00:40:28.190
So maybe we could show you some
excerpts of the first part.

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:33.050
It's a rare Stravinsky
piece that's rarely done.

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:37.400
And so there's a
lot of hand shadows,

00:40:37.400 --> 00:40:39.350
and that's all
done live on stage.

00:40:39.350 --> 00:40:40.829
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:40:40.829 --> 00:40:43.787
[OPERA MUSIC PLAYING]

00:41:21.947 --> 00:41:22.530
[END PLAYBACK]

00:41:22.530 --> 00:41:24.238
And the audience gets
to see both, right?

00:41:24.238 --> 00:41:25.050
Yeah, absolutely.

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:29.340
Because that's one of the things
that I think is interesting.

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:30.840
Because yesterday,
I part of a panel

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:34.858
about the world of illusions,
and theatrical illusions,

00:41:34.858 --> 00:41:35.400
and all that.

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:37.260
And how one of the
important things

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.840
about any kind of illusion,
or the use of technologies,

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.660
is to actually have
confidence in the audience

00:41:42.660 --> 00:41:43.650
and their intelligence.

00:41:43.650 --> 00:41:47.730
And actually show them
how things are made.

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:51.870
So people actually see on
the side all of the hand

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:52.860
shadow and all that.

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:55.920
But, of course, the
human eye is always

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:57.810
more interested in fantasy.

00:41:57.810 --> 00:42:01.260
So you eventually always
look for the fantastical.

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:05.790
I did a show once called
The Far Side of the Moon

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:08.370
where I would kind of
crawl on the floor,

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:12.090
and I had a mirror
in diagonal, and you

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:15.115
would get the illusion that I
was actually floating in space.

00:42:15.115 --> 00:42:16.740
And of course, all
of this was exposed.

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:19.320
The audience could see me
dragging myself on the floor,

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:21.600
and they would see the
effect up in the mirror.

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:24.240
And even though they
would see how I do it,

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:27.430
they would always be
drawn by the effect.

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:33.960
So, you know, I trust
the audience thirst

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:36.180
for fantasy and for poetry.

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:38.470
I think it's marvelous
the way you do combine

00:42:38.470 --> 00:42:41.760
sort of low tech and new.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:45.240
I remember when we were first
talking about the Ring, when

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:48.000
I was pursuing
you in London, you

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:49.920
were doing an opera at
the Royal Opera House.

00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:51.990
Or rather, it was after that.

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:54.000
You were doing the
Anderson Project in London,

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:55.770
and I went to watch that.

00:42:55.770 --> 00:42:59.250
It was a wonderful one-man
show, like other one-man shows

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:01.410
you've done, where you
play multiple characters.

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:05.310
And at one point
in the show, you

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:10.320
walk across the stage as one
character, pass behind a tree,

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:11.730
and then emerge
on the other side

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:14.700
without breaking stride
dressed completely differently

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:16.298
as another character.

00:43:16.298 --> 00:43:18.090
Yes, those are all old
tricks of the trade.

00:43:18.090 --> 00:43:18.470
All--

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:20.670
I remember saying to you
after, how did you do that?

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:22.750
You said, oh, that's
an 18th century--

00:43:22.750 --> 00:43:23.430
Yeah.

00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:28.080
It's actually a
body double who's

00:43:28.080 --> 00:43:31.260
hiding in the tree
who actually--

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:35.730
and then I get to have a glass
of water and I hide a while.

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:37.720
So they're all 19th
century tricks.

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:38.970
So I'm very, very--

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:43.140
I'm fascinated by that,
because I think that we're

00:43:43.140 --> 00:43:44.650
in a world of razzmatazz.

00:43:44.650 --> 00:43:48.480
We go to see 3D films
and we watch television.

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:51.480
And everything is into special
effects, and all of that.

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:55.530
And very often, you're a bit
alienated by all of that,

00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:57.630
because you always go,
how did they do that?

00:43:57.630 --> 00:43:58.740
Or how do I do that?

00:43:58.740 --> 00:43:59.573
I didn't understand.

00:43:59.573 --> 00:44:00.420
How did he do that?

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:01.920
But when you're in
the theater, you

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:04.680
have the possibility to
actually show the strings

00:44:04.680 --> 00:44:07.650
and show how it's done, and
give a chance to the audience

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:10.260
to use their intelligence
and their creativity.

00:44:10.260 --> 00:44:14.670
And even if they do find how
you do that, it actually, it's

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:17.040
part of the enjoyment
of the whole thing.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:21.540
When we were talking
about the Ring at first,

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:28.320
I remember you told me
that you had recently

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:31.912
produced, directed, this
new show in Las Vegas.

00:44:31.912 --> 00:44:33.870
Was that your first or
second Cirque du Soleil?

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:35.060
It was the first show I
did for Cirque du Soleil.

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:37.350
First show you did for
Cirque du Soleil called KA.

00:44:37.350 --> 00:44:40.920
Which you suggested I might
want to go and check out,

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:42.780
because you told me
that you were going

00:44:42.780 --> 00:44:46.200
to use some of the
technological ideas

00:44:46.200 --> 00:44:49.740
that you developed for that
in the service of the Ring.

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:50.760
Yeah.

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:52.480
And that we would maybe--

00:44:52.480 --> 00:44:54.480
maybe Cirque du Soleil
shouldn't hear this-- but

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:56.005
that, basically,
we were going to,

00:44:56.005 --> 00:44:57.630
the Met was going to
be the beneficiary

00:44:57.630 --> 00:45:05.100
of this $200 million commercial
project in Las Vegas, which

00:45:05.100 --> 00:45:06.828
had the most amazing effects.

00:45:06.828 --> 00:45:08.370
Obviously, was a
whole different type

00:45:08.370 --> 00:45:10.200
of pop culture kind of event.

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:15.105
But some of that technology in
terms of motion control and--

00:45:15.105 --> 00:45:16.710
And also interactivity.

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:20.700
--interactivity was, you
developed for that show,

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:23.530
and then used it
now in the Ring.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:26.460
And actually, I
think in the Ring,

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:28.933
the technology caught
up with the ideas,

00:45:28.933 --> 00:45:29.850
which is kind of nice.

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:31.630
But in the days of
Cirque Du Soleil,

00:45:31.630 --> 00:45:33.660
there were still very,
very bulky ideas.

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:37.330
And I know we have some clips
of some of these things.

00:45:37.330 --> 00:45:39.390
Maybe we could
show, there's the--

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:41.310
maybe the battle scene--

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:42.180
From KA?

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:42.680
From KA.

00:45:42.680 --> 00:45:44.138
[INTERPOSING VOICES]
We could maybe

00:45:44.138 --> 00:45:46.800
show that, where we tried
to use interactive video

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:49.320
for the first time and
some of these effects

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:51.426
have found themselves in, uh.

00:45:51.426 --> 00:45:53.410
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:57.378
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:46:22.377 --> 00:46:22.960
[END PLAYBACK]

00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:26.260
So, of course, this looks like
an overhead shot of a battle.

00:46:26.260 --> 00:46:28.930
But actually, the whole
stage is vertical,

00:46:28.930 --> 00:46:31.700
and they're actually the
thing is happening like this.

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:32.290
And--

00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:34.030
So can you explain,
how does it work?

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:38.410
Well, actually, they all have
a harness, and in their hand

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:40.750
they have a little button.

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:43.350
And they actually
pull themselves up

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:44.350
with this little button.

00:46:44.350 --> 00:46:46.360
They push on the button
and it determines the speed

00:46:46.360 --> 00:46:47.680
at which they go up and down.

00:46:47.680 --> 00:46:51.160
And they, so they're
completely independent of the--

00:46:51.160 --> 00:46:53.120
But they're being tracked
by infrared sensors?

00:46:53.120 --> 00:46:57.035
And then what you see at the
bottom, the water ripples

00:46:57.035 --> 00:47:02.710
and all of that, actually the
floor is a sensitive floor

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:05.785
that we created, that
we invented at Cirque du

00:47:05.785 --> 00:47:08.380
Soleil in 2003, I think it was.

00:47:08.380 --> 00:47:11.020
So that actually,
the wall in which

00:47:11.020 --> 00:47:13.600
you're crashing to do
this knows at what speed

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:16.580
you're coming in, at what
velocity, where you are.

00:47:16.580 --> 00:47:18.850
So the video actually
reacts to you.

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:21.000
But, I mean, the
point I wanted to make

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:22.645
with this excerpt is that--

00:47:22.645 --> 00:47:24.980
and the same thing
for the Met-- is

00:47:24.980 --> 00:47:26.980
that all of these
technologies and these effects

00:47:26.980 --> 00:47:31.610
are there to be an echo of
whatever the performer does.

00:47:31.610 --> 00:47:34.360
The thing is not to try
to draw the attention.

00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:37.630
These effects don't draw
the attention to themselves.

00:47:37.630 --> 00:47:40.600
The idea is to try
to magnify whatever

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:43.810
the performers, the
velocity, the strength.

00:47:43.810 --> 00:47:48.100
And in the Ring, there's
actually some great moments

00:47:48.100 --> 00:47:52.030
where what people see on stage,
let's say it's a snowstorm

00:47:52.030 --> 00:47:54.940
or if it's the wind that's
in the trees, all of that

00:47:54.940 --> 00:47:58.570
is triggered by the sound
that comes from the pit.

00:47:58.570 --> 00:48:02.710
And depending of the sibilance,
the dynamics, the velocity,

00:48:02.710 --> 00:48:04.270
whatever happens that night--

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:06.440
Almost all the singers
in the Ring are mic'd.

00:48:06.440 --> 00:48:06.940
Yeah.

00:48:06.940 --> 00:48:09.010
Not to project their
voices into the theater,

00:48:09.010 --> 00:48:11.560
but to help interact
with the scenic effects

00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:11.710
[INTERPOSING VOICES].

00:48:11.710 --> 00:48:12.210
Exactly.

00:48:12.210 --> 00:48:15.130
So a lot of the
effects in the ring

00:48:15.130 --> 00:48:19.330
are triggered by whatever the
performance is that night.

00:48:19.330 --> 00:48:23.080
And so, of course,
it's very tricky,

00:48:23.080 --> 00:48:25.580
because you always have to find
the balance in these things.

00:48:25.580 --> 00:48:27.580
And you don't want this
effect to draw attention

00:48:27.580 --> 00:48:28.223
to themselves.

00:48:28.223 --> 00:48:29.890
But of course, there's
always that risk,

00:48:29.890 --> 00:48:32.307
as people have never seen these
things for the first time.

00:48:32.307 --> 00:48:34.000
They, of course, they--

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:37.960
I know last night, after the
showing of the film at Tribeca,

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:41.710
Jay Hunter Morris, who was this
miraculous discovery who was

00:48:41.710 --> 00:48:43.450
a cover for the
role of Siegfried--

00:48:43.450 --> 00:48:46.090
possibly the hardest tenor
role in all of opera--

00:48:46.090 --> 00:48:48.940
who stepped in three
days before the premiere,

00:48:48.940 --> 00:48:53.500
because the tenor who was
supposed to sing it wasn't well

00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:55.690
and couldn't go on.

00:48:55.690 --> 00:48:57.732
And Jay was saying last
night that he realized,

00:48:57.732 --> 00:48:59.440
because he was not
familiar with the set,

00:48:59.440 --> 00:49:01.000
he had to get
familiar very fast.

00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:05.200
So as he was walking across
the set in the love duet

00:49:05.200 --> 00:49:08.620
with Brunhilde that takes
place at the end of Siegfried

00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:12.820
when he discovers her, and
he's the hero who unlocks her

00:49:12.820 --> 00:49:16.690
from the sleep that she's been
sentenced to by her father,

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:18.410
he realized that
as he was walking,

00:49:18.410 --> 00:49:22.630
leaves were moving around
the stage that were--

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:23.470
Virtual leaves.

00:49:23.470 --> 00:49:25.190
--interactive virtual leaves.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:27.854
And when he kicked one, it
actually moved out of the way.

00:49:27.854 --> 00:49:30.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:30.580 --> 00:49:34.000
So he was surprised.

00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:36.820
He was surprised as
he interacted himself.

00:49:36.820 --> 00:49:39.520
Which also, I think, added to
the wonder of his performance,

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:40.180
perhaps.

00:49:40.180 --> 00:49:41.597
Well, that's an
interesting thing.

00:49:41.597 --> 00:49:45.670
Because actually, when you do
get to work with the singers,

00:49:45.670 --> 00:49:47.500
and some of them,
of course, are so

00:49:47.500 --> 00:49:54.010
preoccupied by being heard, and
being there where they should

00:49:54.010 --> 00:49:57.820
be in the score, and having
eye contact with the conductor.

00:49:57.820 --> 00:50:02.050
And also trying to perform and
trying to be a good listener.

00:50:02.050 --> 00:50:07.180
So an opera singer's mind is
very, very busy when he sings.

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:09.730
And you know, you try
to say, well, there's

00:50:09.730 --> 00:50:12.160
also all this world around
you that if ever you

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:14.680
want to use it, use it.

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:16.930
So, of course, some singers,
and certainly the singers

00:50:16.930 --> 00:50:19.360
who sing the part
for the first time

00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:22.690
will be less generous
with you on that aspect.

00:50:22.690 --> 00:50:27.010
But people like Bryn Terfel and
[INTERPOSING VOICES] who know

00:50:27.010 --> 00:50:31.030
the part so well and [INAUDIBLE]
great would climb on the set

00:50:31.030 --> 00:50:33.130
when it's not time to
climb on it, and would do,

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:34.450
say I want to ride this.

00:50:34.450 --> 00:50:35.360
I want to ride it.

00:50:35.360 --> 00:50:39.318
And actually have fun with
the interaction and the video

00:50:39.318 --> 00:50:39.860
and all that.

00:50:39.860 --> 00:50:44.260
So I think with
time, people will

00:50:44.260 --> 00:50:47.950
get used to these new tools
and these new possibilities,

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:48.790
I think.

00:50:48.790 --> 00:50:51.790
So right now, I wasn't
really aware of this

00:50:51.790 --> 00:50:56.440
until I asked to see what
you were doing at the moment,

00:50:56.440 --> 00:50:57.910
and in the world.

00:50:57.910 --> 00:51:03.430
You have-- there's a
list here somewhere.

00:51:03.430 --> 00:51:05.740
You actually have,
this very moment, four

00:51:05.740 --> 00:51:07.330
different theatrical shows.

00:51:07.330 --> 00:51:10.000
Lip Sync, Blue Dragon,
the Anderson Project,

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:13.690
and a new show called Spades
that I don't know about.

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:16.390
Which all of them are on tour.

00:51:16.390 --> 00:51:19.180
In the case of Spades,
it's opening in two weeks.

00:51:19.180 --> 00:51:20.920
You have the Ring
playing at the Met.

00:51:20.920 --> 00:51:26.450
You have Nightingale, Rossignol,
possibly playing in Lyon

00:51:26.450 --> 00:51:29.640
if the strikers
come back to work.

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:34.440
You have two Cirque Du Soleil
shows playing simultaneously.

00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:35.420
One is TOTEM.

00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:38.330
TOTEM is coming
to Boston in June.

00:51:38.330 --> 00:51:41.840
You have your annual
architectural visual project,

00:51:41.840 --> 00:51:43.580
the Image Mill.

00:51:43.580 --> 00:51:46.730
So it's no wonder you're
hard to get hold of.

00:51:50.100 --> 00:51:55.220
And then in the creative
oven right now, you have--

00:51:55.220 --> 00:51:57.212
what do you have-- another show.

00:51:57.212 --> 00:51:58.670
Maybe you could
explain what Spades

00:51:58.670 --> 00:52:00.337
is, because there's
another playing card

00:52:00.337 --> 00:52:01.850
show called Diamonds.

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:03.080
Which is--

00:52:03.080 --> 00:52:05.960
Well, actually, Spades is
part of the playing card--

00:52:05.960 --> 00:52:07.090
Right.

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:08.760
And the Tempest at the Met--

00:52:08.760 --> 00:52:09.590
And the Tempest.

00:52:09.590 --> 00:52:12.230
--a contemporary
opera by Thomas Ades

00:52:12.230 --> 00:52:15.950
that has already had two
productions since its premiere

00:52:15.950 --> 00:52:17.840
in London 2004 or 2005.

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:18.410
Four, yeah.

00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:21.758
And which you just
were working on--

00:52:21.758 --> 00:52:22.300
Workshopping.

00:52:22.300 --> 00:52:23.270
Workshopping last week.

00:52:23.270 --> 00:52:23.780
Close to Montreal.

00:52:23.780 --> 00:52:23.930
Yeah.

00:52:23.930 --> 00:52:25.370
So, so this is quite exciting.

00:52:25.370 --> 00:52:27.120
So how do you do all
these things at once?

00:52:27.120 --> 00:52:30.260
Well, I'm very well organized,
very well structured.

00:52:30.260 --> 00:52:33.350
I have-- I'm a very,
very chaotic person.

00:52:33.350 --> 00:52:35.140
The way, I work in a
very intuitive way.

00:52:35.140 --> 00:52:35.640
So.

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:38.210
Nobody is doing-- has
this many shows going on

00:52:38.210 --> 00:52:38.990
at the same time.

00:52:38.990 --> 00:52:39.715
Well, maybe--

00:52:39.715 --> 00:52:40.340
That I know of.

00:52:40.340 --> 00:52:42.400
--Bob Wilson probably
has a few of those.

00:52:42.400 --> 00:52:43.400
And I could name a few--

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:44.570
He's moved at a slower pace.

00:52:44.570 --> 00:52:47.160
OK, yeah, of course.

00:52:47.160 --> 00:52:48.507
For some reason, as I get older.

00:52:48.507 --> 00:52:49.590
I get more and more stuff.

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:50.120
Like, no.

00:52:50.120 --> 00:52:51.537
But the thing that
has to be known

00:52:51.537 --> 00:52:57.000
is that I work in a
very, very intuitive way.

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:59.870
I don't work in an intellectual
organized way and all that.

00:52:59.870 --> 00:53:02.100
But I have tons of people
around me to help me out.

00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:05.750
But it all takes place in where
Robert works, his headquarters,

00:53:05.750 --> 00:53:08.420
which you developed
in the '90s, I guess,

00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:11.330
is this converted
firehouse barracks.

00:53:11.330 --> 00:53:13.410
It was a firehouse and
the sleeping quarters--

00:53:13.410 --> 00:53:13.910
Yeah.

00:53:13.910 --> 00:53:15.190
--called La...?

00:53:15.190 --> 00:53:15.935
La Caserne.

00:53:15.935 --> 00:53:17.090
La Caserne, which
means that, I guess.

00:53:17.090 --> 00:53:17.590
Yeah.

00:53:17.590 --> 00:53:20.570
Where all the creative
stuff happens.

00:53:20.570 --> 00:53:21.650
Yeah, pretty much.

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:23.070
That's the starting place.

00:53:23.070 --> 00:53:23.570
So.

00:53:23.570 --> 00:53:25.903
So how many people do you
have in there doing something?

00:53:25.903 --> 00:53:28.100
Well, maybe we have at
La Caserne, maybe 20

00:53:28.100 --> 00:53:29.390
some people who are permanent.

00:53:29.390 --> 00:53:32.000
But there's, like, hundreds of
people that work there a year.

00:53:32.000 --> 00:53:35.270
And we work also with, we
co-produce and exchange

00:53:35.270 --> 00:53:37.250
with a lot of people
around the world.

00:53:37.250 --> 00:53:39.980
But I think the
idea-- and not saying

00:53:39.980 --> 00:53:42.352
there's a secret recipe
to be able to do so

00:53:42.352 --> 00:53:43.560
many things at the same time.

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:46.010
It's just that you have to
be not only well-supported,

00:53:46.010 --> 00:53:49.550
well-structured,
well-organized by extremely

00:53:49.550 --> 00:53:51.710
intelligent and structured
people around you.

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:55.440
But you also have to leave
time for things to happen.

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:58.640
So for example, when we started
working on the Ring five, six

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:02.030
years ago, and you work on
the Ring for maybe two weeks,

00:54:02.030 --> 00:54:06.145
and then you let it
there for four months.

00:54:06.145 --> 00:54:08.270
And in the meantime, you're
working on a new opera,

00:54:08.270 --> 00:54:09.800
or on another show,
on a creation,

00:54:09.800 --> 00:54:11.180
or on the circus show.

00:54:11.180 --> 00:54:13.890
And they also go through
the same kind of process.

00:54:13.890 --> 00:54:18.530
So after a while, you can
actually manage a lot of things

00:54:18.530 --> 00:54:19.240
at the same time.

00:54:19.240 --> 00:54:24.950
And they kind of cook, and
it's slow food, they call it.

00:54:24.950 --> 00:54:27.230
So I'm very privileged
now because I'm

00:54:27.230 --> 00:54:29.360
in a position where
I never say yes

00:54:29.360 --> 00:54:31.580
to a project that's going
to happen this summer

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:32.390
or in the fall.

00:54:32.390 --> 00:54:34.520
I say, OK, and what year?

00:54:34.520 --> 00:54:39.380
And how can we space out the
development phases of that

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:39.990
project?

00:54:39.990 --> 00:54:42.380
And I think that's
part of the secret.

00:54:42.380 --> 00:54:46.010
Because a lot of stuff happens
in the back of your head

00:54:46.010 --> 00:54:48.830
that you don't know about
between these sessions.

00:54:48.830 --> 00:54:51.000
You work on, let's
say, we'd rehearse

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:53.000
for three weeks on a
project, and then everybody

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:54.110
disappears for four months.

00:54:54.110 --> 00:54:55.100
And during that
four months, you're

00:54:55.100 --> 00:54:56.630
not thinking of the project.

00:54:56.630 --> 00:54:58.500
But something in the
back of your head,

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:01.505
your subconscious actually
mends stuff, finds solutions.

00:55:01.505 --> 00:55:02.630
So that when we come back--

00:55:02.630 --> 00:55:03.922
So that's good for the creator.

00:55:03.922 --> 00:55:04.640
Absolutely, yeah.

00:55:04.640 --> 00:55:08.070
And all of these projects
inform each other.

00:55:08.070 --> 00:55:13.880
So sometimes the answer to
a problem on one project

00:55:13.880 --> 00:55:16.080
will be found in the other
project and all that.

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:18.470
So that's also a very
intricate system.

00:55:18.470 --> 00:55:19.820
And you can't command that.

00:55:19.820 --> 00:55:20.570
That happens.

00:55:20.570 --> 00:55:23.807
It just happens that way.

00:55:23.807 --> 00:55:25.640
Do you want to, are
there any other examples

00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:27.200
of current stuff that you--

00:55:27.200 --> 00:55:28.100
I don't know.

00:55:28.100 --> 00:55:30.050
--you're doing that
you'd like to talk about?

00:55:30.050 --> 00:55:32.120
How about the Image Mill?

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:36.680
Which is this annual
visual event that

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:38.010
started a couple of years ago.

00:55:38.010 --> 00:55:39.200
It's in Quebec City.

00:55:39.200 --> 00:55:39.710
Yeah.

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:40.210
Where you--

00:55:40.210 --> 00:55:42.410
So at this place that you
were mentioning earlier

00:55:42.410 --> 00:55:46.760
on the Caserne, which is the
fire hall or studio or lab

00:55:46.760 --> 00:55:50.540
in Quebec City where we
develop most of our things,

00:55:50.540 --> 00:55:53.120
we have a lot of
interesting creative minds

00:55:53.120 --> 00:55:54.770
that gravitate around us.

00:55:54.770 --> 00:55:56.540
It feels, at times,
a bit like MIT.

00:55:56.540 --> 00:56:00.110
And that's why I feel a bit
at home here in that sense.

00:56:00.110 --> 00:56:02.000
We have some really
interesting minds.

00:56:02.000 --> 00:56:06.560
And so the Image
Mill was created

00:56:06.560 --> 00:56:12.360
during the 2008 celebrations of
the 400th anniversary of Quebec

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:12.860
City.

00:56:12.860 --> 00:56:15.980
So I had been commissioned
to do some kind of event,

00:56:15.980 --> 00:56:18.770
and we chose to do
the world's largest

00:56:18.770 --> 00:56:20.150
architectural projection.

00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:23.890
It's actually 0.6
kilometers long.

00:56:23.890 --> 00:56:25.500
It's in the Guinness
Book of Records.

00:56:25.500 --> 00:56:28.630
Well, if I'm going to brag
about something, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:56:28.630 --> 00:56:33.050
And, well, maybe we
could show some images

00:56:33.050 --> 00:56:37.750
as we're speaking, just to give
you an idea of the scope of it.

00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:40.820
Or the, and what it is.

00:56:40.820 --> 00:56:43.510
So here you see it's in
the harbor in Quebec City.

00:56:43.510 --> 00:56:48.520
And those are 86 grain
silos, the building

00:56:48.520 --> 00:56:49.930
that you see there.

00:56:49.930 --> 00:56:51.910
It's this huge, huge,
huge, huge thing

00:56:51.910 --> 00:56:53.350
that's actually
blocking the view

00:56:53.350 --> 00:56:54.740
from the river in Quebec City.

00:56:54.740 --> 00:57:00.460
So we decided to transform it
into some kind of projection

00:57:00.460 --> 00:57:01.180
surface.

00:57:01.180 --> 00:57:06.260
And we managed, with time, to
add 3D effects and all that.

00:57:06.260 --> 00:57:09.070
So this is a result of
projections that we do.

00:57:09.070 --> 00:57:12.520
We pretend that the silos
actually reveal their content,

00:57:12.520 --> 00:57:17.690
and it's quite a big-- so this
is in the context of the city.

00:57:17.690 --> 00:57:19.340
You see how big it is.

00:57:19.340 --> 00:57:22.270
And so it's seen from
every point in the city,

00:57:22.270 --> 00:57:29.020
and people gather in
the harbor to see it.

00:57:29.020 --> 00:57:31.270
But also, it's seen
from hotel rooms,

00:57:31.270 --> 00:57:37.270
and its soundtrack is on a
radio channel that people can,

00:57:37.270 --> 00:57:38.500
so it's quite a big event.

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:39.820
People come by boat.

00:57:39.820 --> 00:57:46.660
There's also people that visit
the river during the daytime,

00:57:46.660 --> 00:57:52.113
and then they all kind of
park their boats in the harbor

00:57:52.113 --> 00:57:52.780
and come to see.

00:57:52.780 --> 00:57:54.940
And, basically, what
it does is the history

00:57:54.940 --> 00:57:59.140
of Quebec City, which is the
oldest city in North America,

00:57:59.140 --> 00:57:59.700
in fact.

00:57:59.700 --> 00:58:06.655
And so it's the story of four
different centuries, the four

00:58:06.655 --> 00:58:08.080
centuries and what
they're about.

00:58:08.080 --> 00:58:09.550
So there's a lot of archives--

00:58:09.550 --> 00:58:11.500
How many projectors do you--

00:58:11.500 --> 00:58:13.210
It's done with 20--

00:58:13.210 --> 00:58:17.522
in fact, it's 25 high
definition projectors,

00:58:17.522 --> 00:58:19.480
but there's probably 28,
because there's always

00:58:19.480 --> 00:58:20.680
one that goes wrong.

00:58:20.680 --> 00:58:22.160
It's always-- you
know about that.

00:58:22.160 --> 00:58:24.702
There's always something that
goes wrong at these big things.

00:58:24.702 --> 00:58:27.690
And it's all synchronized
by the same computer.

00:58:27.690 --> 00:58:29.800
So it's a big team that
works on that every year,

00:58:29.800 --> 00:58:32.530
and it's quite an
event, and it's

00:58:32.530 --> 00:58:35.120
part of the signature
of the city now.

00:58:35.120 --> 00:58:36.340
That's fantastic.

00:58:36.340 --> 00:58:42.010
Of course, the downside of
using all this technology

00:58:42.010 --> 00:58:45.730
is that when things do go wrong,
you feel a little bit helpless.

00:58:45.730 --> 00:58:49.420
[LAUGHING] Yeah, of course.

00:58:49.420 --> 00:58:52.570
We've had our-- I
mean, but that's OK.

00:58:52.570 --> 00:58:54.070
That's part of the
sport of theater.

00:58:54.070 --> 00:58:55.112
That's always what I say.

00:58:55.112 --> 00:58:59.800
I mean, just because we got
accustomed to the guillotine

00:58:59.800 --> 00:59:01.450
of the opening night of a movie.

00:59:01.450 --> 00:59:02.830
You can't change a movie.

00:59:02.830 --> 00:59:04.925
The movie's out, it's
there, distributed.

00:59:04.925 --> 00:59:05.800
We can't do anything.

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:07.930
But theater, it's
a living animal.

00:59:07.930 --> 00:59:08.620
And it changes.

00:59:08.620 --> 00:59:09.990
And of course, some evenings--

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:12.490
[INTERPOSING VOICES] the Ring
has changed the entire culture

00:59:12.490 --> 00:59:15.550
and atmosphere of the
Met in terms of inside

00:59:15.550 --> 00:59:17.560
the Met is the stage crew.

00:59:17.560 --> 00:59:21.880
We have 200 stagehands
who work at the Met

00:59:21.880 --> 00:59:24.970
in order to move the
scenery in and out of

00:59:24.970 --> 00:59:27.160
and to operate shows.

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:29.710
We're a repertory
theater with seven

00:59:29.710 --> 00:59:34.630
shows a week, two different
operas on Saturday, matinee

00:59:34.630 --> 00:59:36.070
and an evening performance.

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:39.175
And a stage that is in
constant use, where we have

00:59:39.175 --> 00:59:40.300
a rehearsal in the daytime.

00:59:40.300 --> 00:59:42.040
Today, for example,
we were rehearsing

00:59:42.040 --> 00:59:43.840
a revival of Billy Budd.

00:59:43.840 --> 00:59:47.140
And now, right at
this moment, the crew

00:59:47.140 --> 00:59:50.020
is frantically making sure
that all the connections

00:59:50.020 --> 00:59:52.330
in the machine are ready
for tonight's performance

00:59:52.330 --> 00:59:53.260
of Rheingold.

00:59:53.260 --> 00:59:56.410
But what's different
about the Ring in terms

00:59:56.410 --> 01:00:00.412
of the culture of the Met
and the way the crew works

01:00:00.412 --> 01:00:01.870
is that for the
first time, they've

01:00:01.870 --> 01:00:06.220
never had to interact
with scenery like this.

01:00:06.220 --> 01:00:08.230
Because as computerized
and advanced

01:00:08.230 --> 01:00:16.000
as the Ring is, ultimately
as you evolve the designs,

01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:21.760
it came down to stagehands
also manipulating the planks

01:00:21.760 --> 01:00:23.830
and holding the
planks and having

01:00:23.830 --> 01:00:26.500
to, in a lot of the
stage, lifts of the Met

01:00:26.500 --> 01:00:30.190
being used to introduce
various scenic elements.

01:00:30.190 --> 01:00:35.470
So it's interactive between
people and the machinery.

01:00:35.470 --> 01:00:38.350
And it's become, also, a bit
puppet-like in a certain way.

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:39.850
It's puppetry.

01:00:39.850 --> 01:00:42.490
And I have to say that
the crew at the Met

01:00:42.490 --> 01:00:45.848
have been so proud
of the whole project.

01:00:45.848 --> 01:00:46.390
They love it.

01:00:46.390 --> 01:00:48.580
And they've invested
themselves so much into this,

01:00:48.580 --> 01:00:51.050
and they're so proud of
the whole production.

01:00:51.050 --> 01:00:53.620
And they've put so much
creative energy and--

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:55.990
Well, they love-- they've
never been so stimulated.

01:00:55.990 --> 01:00:59.530
I mean, because every stagehand
gets to really be involved--

01:00:59.530 --> 01:01:00.250
Absolutely.

01:01:00.250 --> 01:01:02.350
--in a way they
normally wouldn't be.

01:01:02.350 --> 01:01:03.798
Yeah.

01:01:03.798 --> 01:01:05.590
I thought it would be
interesting, perhaps,

01:01:05.590 --> 01:01:06.520
to talk a little bit.

01:01:06.520 --> 01:01:12.340
Once you said to me, I remember
some months ago that you,

01:01:12.340 --> 01:01:14.320
I don't know if you
were actually a busker.

01:01:14.320 --> 01:01:18.370
But you said that
you grew up with sort

01:01:18.370 --> 01:01:23.260
of this uniquely gifted
group of Quebecois citizens

01:01:23.260 --> 01:01:25.930
who all sort of come
from the same generation,

01:01:25.930 --> 01:01:27.480
including Guy--

01:01:27.480 --> 01:01:28.110
Guy Laliberte.

01:01:28.110 --> 01:01:31.650
Guy Laliberte, the founder
of Cirque Du Soleil.

01:01:31.650 --> 01:01:33.130
So is that, how did you begin?

01:01:33.130 --> 01:01:33.630
I mean--

01:01:33.630 --> 01:01:38.130
Well, actually, I studied
at the conservatory

01:01:38.130 --> 01:01:40.030
to become an actor.

01:01:40.030 --> 01:01:40.530
But--

01:01:40.530 --> 01:01:41.570
In Canada?

01:01:41.570 --> 01:01:42.510
In Quebec City.

01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:47.910
And so when I came out
of the conservatory,

01:01:47.910 --> 01:01:50.485
I didn't think I'd have
a career as an actor,

01:01:50.485 --> 01:01:52.860
because I didn't have any
casting for anything, you know.

01:01:52.860 --> 01:01:56.330
And in those days, it was very,
very kind of flower power.

01:01:56.330 --> 01:01:59.190
And we were very much
into collective writing

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:01.080
and doing political
theater and all that.

01:02:01.080 --> 01:02:04.365
So I was pretty much
doing that kind of thing.

01:02:04.365 --> 01:02:06.240
We would do stuff and
we'd play in the street

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:08.490
and we'd make the money
that people would give us.

01:02:08.490 --> 01:02:11.367
And it was very influenced
by the Bread and Puppet

01:02:11.367 --> 01:02:13.200
and the Living Theater
and whatever movement

01:02:13.200 --> 01:02:15.210
was going on here in
the United States.

01:02:15.210 --> 01:02:16.860
And never really
thought that I'd

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:20.620
have a career as an
actor or a director.

01:02:20.620 --> 01:02:23.663
And, you know, just I always
did this small little troupes

01:02:23.663 --> 01:02:25.080
and didn't have
any big ambitions.

01:02:25.080 --> 01:02:27.780
And, eventually, it
kind of caught on,

01:02:27.780 --> 01:02:29.850
and we started
touring the world.

01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:32.100
And people start to be very
interested in our work.

01:02:32.100 --> 01:02:34.498
Touring on your own,
or with a group?

01:02:34.498 --> 01:02:35.040
With a group.

01:02:35.040 --> 01:02:35.940
With a company.

01:02:35.940 --> 01:02:42.900
And for some reason, our
work moved and interested,

01:02:42.900 --> 01:02:45.780
and people identified ourselves
to what we were doing.

01:02:45.780 --> 01:02:50.070
And suddenly, we started
co-producing with people

01:02:50.070 --> 01:02:53.670
from all over the world, and
we became very much in demand.

01:02:53.670 --> 01:02:58.200
And but I always remembered that
when I was in the conservatory,

01:02:58.200 --> 01:02:59.910
I was interested in
acting and all that.

01:02:59.910 --> 01:03:03.030
But I spent way too much
time with the people

01:03:03.030 --> 01:03:06.330
doing production I was very
interested in sound editing

01:03:06.330 --> 01:03:08.560
and lighting design and
all these other things.

01:03:08.560 --> 01:03:11.730
And I'd always get flak from--

01:03:11.730 --> 01:03:14.310
my teachers would say you're
spending way too much time

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:17.248
on these things, and you should
be working more on your acting,

01:03:17.248 --> 01:03:17.790
and all that.

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:20.190
So from the very,
very beginning I've

01:03:20.190 --> 01:03:23.100
always had a finger
in every pie,

01:03:23.100 --> 01:03:27.540
and I never thought that that
would actually be a quality.

01:03:27.540 --> 01:03:29.820
It's always been seen as a flaw.

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:32.950
And so with time--

01:03:32.950 --> 01:03:36.990
so it creates a kind of
conscience of what theater is

01:03:36.990 --> 01:03:38.070
and how.

01:03:38.070 --> 01:03:40.533
And you have to be, I mean, if
you want theater to survive,

01:03:40.533 --> 01:03:42.450
you have to be interested
in all these things.

01:03:42.450 --> 01:03:43.908
You've not only
worked as an actor,

01:03:43.908 --> 01:03:45.600
you've worked as
a dancer recently.

01:03:45.600 --> 01:03:46.500
Recently.

01:03:46.500 --> 01:03:49.170
At age 50.

01:03:49.170 --> 01:03:52.680
At age 50, Sylvie Guillem,
who's an amazing dancer, who's

01:03:52.680 --> 01:03:57.780
a star dancer, showed up at
a performance of the Anderson

01:03:57.780 --> 01:03:59.970
Project in Australia
and said, well, I'd

01:03:59.970 --> 01:04:01.020
like to work with you.

01:04:01.020 --> 01:04:02.910
And I said fine.

01:04:02.910 --> 01:04:07.350
And but I thought she wanted me
to direct her, or to help her.

01:04:07.350 --> 01:04:11.670
Because I knew she
wanted to maybe find

01:04:11.670 --> 01:04:12.820
a way to become an actress.

01:04:12.820 --> 01:04:14.310
So I said, well, fine.

01:04:14.310 --> 01:04:16.150
And we had a few
meetings and all that.

01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:17.680
And at one point
I said, you know,

01:04:17.680 --> 01:04:20.830
Sylvie, I'm not sure
that I'm the right person

01:04:20.830 --> 01:04:22.830
to work with you because
I'm not a choreographer

01:04:22.830 --> 01:04:24.240
and I'm not sure I
understand very well.

01:04:24.240 --> 01:04:26.543
Well, she said, I don't
want you to choreograph me

01:04:26.543 --> 01:04:27.210
or to direct me.

01:04:27.210 --> 01:04:29.220
I want to dance with you.

01:04:29.220 --> 01:04:31.455
And with Sylvie Guillem--

01:04:31.455 --> 01:04:32.580
You didn't have to say yes.

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:33.198
Well, no.

01:04:33.198 --> 01:04:34.740
But if Sylvie Guillem
comes up to you

01:04:34.740 --> 01:04:36.782
and she says, I want to
dance with you, you know,

01:04:36.782 --> 01:04:39.150
you have to say yes to that.

01:04:39.150 --> 01:04:44.550
So I was at least 35 pounds
larger, bigger, heavier,

01:04:44.550 --> 01:04:45.300
whatever you say.

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:50.820
And I said, my god,
this is suicidal.

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:52.650
But I said, why not?

01:04:52.650 --> 01:04:54.780
And we got together
and developed

01:04:54.780 --> 01:04:58.200
this extraordinary story
about Chevalier [INAUDIBLE],,

01:04:58.200 --> 01:04:59.940
and it's a long and
difficult to explain.

01:04:59.940 --> 01:05:04.920
And it's classical dance,
modern dance, theater,

01:05:04.920 --> 01:05:07.920
influenced by a lot of martial
arts and all these things.

01:05:07.920 --> 01:05:12.150
And a year later,
I was dancing--

01:05:12.150 --> 01:05:15.390
I was dancing at Sadler's
Wells and we just recently

01:05:15.390 --> 01:05:16.860
came back from Japan.

01:05:16.860 --> 01:05:18.000
And--

01:05:18.000 --> 01:05:19.830
It's a good way to
keep your weight down.

01:05:19.830 --> 01:05:20.872
Well, that's the problem.

01:05:20.872 --> 01:05:24.600
Is that I'm a squeezebox.

01:05:24.600 --> 01:05:25.410
I'm an accordion.

01:05:25.410 --> 01:05:27.210
It's just that every
time we do the show,

01:05:27.210 --> 01:05:30.750
I have to lose a lot of weight
to fit in my Alexander McQueen

01:05:30.750 --> 01:05:31.860
tights.

01:05:31.860 --> 01:05:37.680
And after that,
when the show is off

01:05:37.680 --> 01:05:40.050
and there's a six month
period between shows,

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:43.590
I just kind of
become this thing.

01:05:43.590 --> 01:05:45.155
So, but it's extraordinary.

01:05:45.155 --> 01:05:46.530
And it's great
because you think,

01:05:46.530 --> 01:05:49.170
and of course,
Sylvie now is maybe,

01:05:49.170 --> 01:05:51.420
I don't know exactly what
age she is, she's, you know,

01:05:51.420 --> 01:05:53.610
she's not in her 20s anymore.

01:05:53.610 --> 01:05:57.600
And she still
works, and she still

01:05:57.600 --> 01:06:00.720
is an amazing, amazing artist.

01:06:00.720 --> 01:06:04.470
And so it kind of was a
good example for me to say,

01:06:04.470 --> 01:06:07.200
well, you know,
retirement's not an option.

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:09.480
I think you could
always learn at any age.

01:06:09.480 --> 01:06:12.840
And I like being surprised in
my career by things like that.

01:06:12.840 --> 01:06:15.520
Suddenly somebody shows up in
your dressing room and says,

01:06:15.520 --> 01:06:17.190
I want to dance with you.

01:06:17.190 --> 01:06:20.130
And you're 50, and you're out
of breath, and you want to die,

01:06:20.130 --> 01:06:20.880
and you have to--

01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:23.760
so, it's, I mean, I like
life life changing things.

01:06:23.760 --> 01:06:25.260
And the same thing
happened with you

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:27.670
when you showed up at
Covent Garden and said,

01:06:27.670 --> 01:06:28.670
you want to do the Ring?

01:06:28.670 --> 01:06:31.460
And I went, [GROANS]
oh, my gosh.

01:06:31.460 --> 01:06:32.900
And you're, well, yeah, why not?

01:06:32.900 --> 01:06:37.220
Somebody offers you to
climb Mount Everest,

01:06:37.220 --> 01:06:38.150
you have to do it.

01:06:38.150 --> 01:06:39.830
You know?

01:06:39.830 --> 01:06:41.810
So I know.

01:06:41.810 --> 01:06:42.920
I like that.

01:06:42.920 --> 01:06:44.420
I mean, I'm afraid
somebody tonight

01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:46.850
is going to come up to
me and suggest something.

01:06:46.850 --> 01:06:48.020
I don't know, [GROANS].

01:06:48.020 --> 01:06:51.740
But, I don't know.

01:06:51.740 --> 01:06:54.620
It's the kind of thing,
it's the kind of accident

01:06:54.620 --> 01:06:55.640
you like to bump into.

01:06:55.640 --> 01:06:57.350
The kind of thing
that's life changing.

01:06:57.350 --> 01:07:00.260
So what are some of the
future Mount Everests for you?

01:07:00.260 --> 01:07:03.230
I know you have this
extraordinary theater project.

01:07:03.230 --> 01:07:04.820
You're building
a theater, right?

01:07:04.820 --> 01:07:05.630
Yeah, we're building
a theater right now.

01:07:05.630 --> 01:07:06.140
Can you talk about that?

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:06.640
Yeah.

01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:10.070
Because we have the Caserne in
Quebec City, but we just have,

01:07:10.070 --> 01:07:11.690
we just received
part of the funding

01:07:11.690 --> 01:07:14.750
to actually build a
new room in Quebec

01:07:14.750 --> 01:07:18.020
City, an actual theater
where we, let's actually

01:07:18.020 --> 01:07:19.610
design for our work.

01:07:19.610 --> 01:07:24.080
And where we'll also be
presenting maybe small operas,

01:07:24.080 --> 01:07:25.220
circus shows.

01:07:25.220 --> 01:07:28.700
It's going to be, it's going
to, a place called the Diamond.

01:07:28.700 --> 01:07:31.100
And the reason why
it's called the Diamond

01:07:31.100 --> 01:07:32.720
because a diamond
has multiple, it's

01:07:32.720 --> 01:07:34.370
multifaceted, like our work.

01:07:34.370 --> 01:07:38.960
But also because Quebec City
is built on Cape Diamond,

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:41.902
because the French thought that
there was a lot of-- that's

01:07:41.902 --> 01:07:42.860
why they settled there.

01:07:42.860 --> 01:07:45.090
They saw these diamonds,
this shiny stuff.

01:07:45.090 --> 01:07:46.970
Of course, it's not
diamonds at all.

01:07:46.970 --> 01:07:52.490
And also because when I
opened that first century

01:07:52.490 --> 01:07:57.920
20 some years ago, Bob
Wilson was elegant enough

01:07:57.920 --> 01:08:01.280
to send me a video
message where he said,

01:08:01.280 --> 01:08:07.010
our cities need centers,
a diamond in an apple,

01:08:07.010 --> 01:08:09.380
which is a very Bob
Wilson thing to say.

01:08:09.380 --> 01:08:11.000
A diamond in an apple.

01:08:11.000 --> 01:08:11.790
Of course.

01:08:11.790 --> 01:08:15.465
And so he said congratulations
to Robert Lepage's center,

01:08:15.465 --> 01:08:16.340
and blah, blah, blah.

01:08:16.340 --> 01:08:17.965
And I said, well,
this is a nice image.

01:08:17.965 --> 01:08:19.010
A diamond in an apple.

01:08:19.010 --> 01:08:19.910
And it's true.

01:08:19.910 --> 01:08:22.640
I mean, I think that our
cultural centers, whether it's

01:08:22.640 --> 01:08:26.330
the Lincoln Center or
the Met or whatever,

01:08:26.330 --> 01:08:29.500
this place here has to be,
that's what we have to.

01:08:29.500 --> 01:08:32.149
Have to be like a shining jewel.

01:08:32.149 --> 01:08:39.080
And with all of the alchemic
notions of what a jewel is.

01:08:39.080 --> 01:08:42.710
Can you describe some of these
shows you have in the works

01:08:42.710 --> 01:08:43.640
right now?

01:08:43.640 --> 01:08:44.630
There's a lot of them.

01:08:44.630 --> 01:08:46.779
But maybe I'd describe
one because it's very

01:08:46.779 --> 01:08:46.910
[INTERPOSING VOICES].

01:08:46.910 --> 01:08:48.740
But the one about
the playing cards.

01:08:48.740 --> 01:08:52.790
And also has to do with what
I did here this week at MIT.

01:08:52.790 --> 01:08:58.399
Is that part of the
MIT events, because I

01:08:58.399 --> 01:09:00.668
was getting a workshop
with some of the,

01:09:00.668 --> 01:09:02.210
I talked to some of
the students here

01:09:02.210 --> 01:09:04.010
about this theme
of playing cards.

01:09:04.010 --> 01:09:10.830
And so after doing the
Ring, a four part epic,

01:09:10.830 --> 01:09:14.450
I'm doing yet another four
part epic on playing cards.

01:09:14.450 --> 01:09:16.939
And of course, the first
part is called Spades,

01:09:16.939 --> 01:09:20.450
the second is Hearts, the
third one is Diamonds,

01:09:20.450 --> 01:09:23.479
and the last one is Clubs.

01:09:23.479 --> 01:09:26.899
And it's a very, very,
very long project.

01:09:26.899 --> 01:09:28.399
It's going to be
12 hours of theater

01:09:28.399 --> 01:09:29.399
eventually and all that.

01:09:29.399 --> 01:09:33.979
But we're premiering in Madrid,
in two weeks from now, Spades.

01:09:33.979 --> 01:09:36.920
And it's, so, it's
just a group of people

01:09:36.920 --> 01:09:38.840
that got together
and started writing

01:09:38.840 --> 01:09:42.979
all these extraordinary stories
about playing cards, which

01:09:42.979 --> 01:09:47.840
is a very, very inspiring
object or sensitive resource.

01:09:47.840 --> 01:09:48.560
And--

01:09:48.560 --> 01:09:49.880
So it's a collective--

01:09:49.880 --> 01:09:52.069
It's a collective effort.

01:09:52.069 --> 01:09:56.458
So that's pretty much
what I'll be doing when I

01:09:56.458 --> 01:09:57.500
[INAUDIBLE] off tomorrow.

01:09:57.500 --> 01:09:58.550
You're going to Madrid tomorrow?

01:09:58.550 --> 01:10:00.380
Well, I'm, no, I'm
going to Quebec City

01:10:00.380 --> 01:10:04.100
to sort out what we're going
to be doing in Madrid two weeks

01:10:04.100 --> 01:10:04.850
from now.

01:10:04.850 --> 01:10:07.850
But it's a three hour show,
and it's quite extraordinary

01:10:07.850 --> 01:10:13.780
because it's set in Las Vegas
the weekend when Celine Dion

01:10:13.780 --> 01:10:20.390
show premiered at, that was in
like, again, in 2002 or 2003.

01:10:20.390 --> 01:10:23.180
And it's that weekend where
she premiered her show

01:10:23.180 --> 01:10:24.860
at Caesar's Palace.

01:10:24.860 --> 01:10:29.420
And it's the same
weekend when the Allies

01:10:29.420 --> 01:10:31.220
started to bomb Baghdad.

01:10:31.220 --> 01:10:34.550
So it's about two deserts
and about two separate set

01:10:34.550 --> 01:10:37.160
of events that are
interconnected.

01:10:37.160 --> 01:10:38.810
And so, yeah.

01:10:38.810 --> 01:10:41.960
So it's a crazy project.

01:10:41.960 --> 01:10:43.218
Sounds great.

01:10:43.218 --> 01:10:45.260
We have a new production
of Rigoletto next season

01:10:45.260 --> 01:10:46.400
set in Las Vegas.

01:10:46.400 --> 01:10:47.990
Oh, really?

01:10:47.990 --> 01:10:49.940
See, there's ripoffs everywhere.

01:10:49.940 --> 01:10:50.670
1960.

01:10:50.670 --> 01:10:51.170
1960.

01:10:51.170 --> 01:10:56.790
[LAUGHING] Been
there, done that.

01:10:56.790 --> 01:10:57.800
Bought the t-shirt.

01:10:57.800 --> 01:11:01.550
You're not directing
it, don't worry.

01:11:01.550 --> 01:11:04.230
Should we ask the audience
if they have any questions?

01:11:04.230 --> 01:11:04.730
Yep.

01:11:04.730 --> 01:11:09.440
I think that we have
microphones standing by for you

01:11:09.440 --> 01:11:11.000
that will be brought in.

01:11:11.000 --> 01:11:12.530
And Phillip is going to--

01:11:12.530 --> 01:11:14.035
There they are.

01:11:14.035 --> 01:11:14.660
There they are.

01:11:14.660 --> 01:11:17.150
--come to our moderating aid.

01:11:19.934 --> 01:11:22.720
[INAUDIBLE]

01:11:22.720 --> 01:11:25.413
That is, if there
are any questions.

01:11:25.413 --> 01:11:28.330
[INAUDIBLE] need
you to come down.

01:11:28.330 --> 01:11:31.250
Thank you very much.

01:11:31.250 --> 01:11:32.140
Oh.

01:11:32.140 --> 01:11:34.000
Oh, there are some questions.

01:11:34.000 --> 01:11:37.720
And in the MIT tradition, if
you would just state your name,

01:11:37.720 --> 01:11:42.730
and very briefly, who
you are, what you are.

01:11:42.730 --> 01:11:45.220
I'm, hello.

01:11:45.220 --> 01:11:46.240
My name is Rhiannon.

01:11:46.240 --> 01:11:51.220
I'm a member of homo
sapiens sapiens, human.

01:11:51.220 --> 01:11:54.190
First I just want to
say, Monsieur Lepage,

01:11:54.190 --> 01:11:55.900
you're like a rock star to me.

01:11:55.900 --> 01:11:56.980
My kind of people.

01:11:56.980 --> 01:11:58.510
I'm like your groupie right now.

01:11:58.510 --> 01:11:59.020
So.

01:11:59.020 --> 01:12:00.190
Yeah.

01:12:00.190 --> 01:12:01.408
You're my Elvis.

01:12:01.408 --> 01:12:04.130
[LAUGHING] (IMITATING ELVIS)
A-thank you very much.

01:12:04.130 --> 01:12:04.660
No, yeah.

01:12:04.660 --> 01:12:07.000
No hip wiggles
necessary, don't worry.

01:12:07.000 --> 01:12:09.670
My question has to do
with people like me.

01:12:09.670 --> 01:12:12.730
I just graduated from
college with a degree

01:12:12.730 --> 01:12:14.350
in music and technology.

01:12:14.350 --> 01:12:16.400
And theater is my life.

01:12:16.400 --> 01:12:17.830
I'm part of Alpha Psi Omega.

01:12:17.830 --> 01:12:20.860
I love being on stage, off
stage, backstage, building

01:12:20.860 --> 01:12:24.640
the stage, whatever stage.

01:12:24.640 --> 01:12:29.380
But the problem is, I just
graduated college with a music

01:12:29.380 --> 01:12:30.610
and technology degree.

01:12:30.610 --> 01:12:32.300
I don't have any money.

01:12:32.300 --> 01:12:33.480
I don't have any time.

01:12:33.480 --> 01:12:35.230
I'm trying to work,
trying to get a house.

01:12:35.230 --> 01:12:37.870
Real life is scary.

01:12:37.870 --> 01:12:40.930
So my question to you, before
I can win the McDermott Award

01:12:40.930 --> 01:12:46.090
and do the Ring
Cycle, what do I do?

01:12:46.090 --> 01:12:47.352
I'm kind of--

01:12:47.352 --> 01:12:50.970
[LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE]

01:12:50.970 --> 01:12:52.300
--I'm kind of lost here.

01:12:52.300 --> 01:12:54.150
I'm wondering, I
don't have the tools.

01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:54.960
I don't have tools.

01:12:54.960 --> 01:12:57.085
I don't have a workshop,
I don't have the contacts,

01:12:57.085 --> 01:13:00.480
and I don't have the
money to get any of it.

01:13:00.480 --> 01:13:01.590
What's my first step?

01:13:01.590 --> 01:13:02.500
Well, I--

01:13:02.500 --> 01:13:03.000
Save me.

01:13:03.000 --> 01:13:06.780
I have to admit that the
funding, the arts funding here

01:13:06.780 --> 01:13:08.860
in this country is still
a big mystery to me.

01:13:08.860 --> 01:13:10.415
I don't really
know how it works.

01:13:10.415 --> 01:13:12.540
I know there's some very
generous people around who

01:13:12.540 --> 01:13:14.190
could help you out,
but I know also,

01:13:14.190 --> 01:13:15.570
that isn't
necessarily a priority

01:13:15.570 --> 01:13:17.970
for governments or for--

01:13:17.970 --> 01:13:22.050
so that I know that in Canada,
we do have that great advantage

01:13:22.050 --> 01:13:23.610
that we have subsidies.

01:13:23.610 --> 01:13:26.400
But this being said,
my company is not,

01:13:26.400 --> 01:13:28.540
I don't get that much
money from the governments.

01:13:28.540 --> 01:13:32.610
We get nearly 12%
of our of the money

01:13:32.610 --> 01:13:35.430
that we generate
because we actually

01:13:35.430 --> 01:13:37.170
understood very
early in the process

01:13:37.170 --> 01:13:39.870
that we had to go
elsewhere to find it.

01:13:39.870 --> 01:13:45.500
That we traveled and we started
doing adventures with people.

01:13:45.500 --> 01:13:49.800
And there are people who are
ready to risk years in advance

01:13:49.800 --> 01:13:50.703
to work with you.

01:13:50.703 --> 01:13:52.120
They'll give you
the money upfront

01:13:52.120 --> 01:13:54.840
and say, well, when you're
ready, when the work is ready.

01:13:54.840 --> 01:13:58.080
So sometimes when we
can't find the solutions

01:13:58.080 --> 01:14:00.850
in our own surroundings we
have to kind of move around.

01:14:00.850 --> 01:14:04.770
And maybe you have to
emigrate to Canada.

01:14:04.770 --> 01:14:08.430
Or embark on any kind
of venture with people

01:14:08.430 --> 01:14:11.520
abroad and eventually be
recognized as an artist.

01:14:11.520 --> 01:14:14.970
There are theater programs in
companies in this country that

01:14:14.970 --> 01:14:18.358
have apprentice
programs, and if that's--

01:14:18.358 --> 01:14:20.775
there are different ways of
getting your foot in the door.

01:14:20.775 --> 01:14:21.585
Hm, thank you.

01:14:21.585 --> 01:14:22.350
Also will you dance with me?

01:14:22.350 --> 01:14:23.183
Thank you very much.

01:14:23.183 --> 01:14:23.930
Please.

01:14:23.930 --> 01:14:24.850
Yeah.

01:14:24.850 --> 01:14:25.350
Hi.

01:14:25.350 --> 01:14:26.910
My name's Matt Gray.

01:14:26.910 --> 01:14:29.700
I actually teach acting
and directing here

01:14:29.700 --> 01:14:31.980
at Northeastern
University, and I also

01:14:31.980 --> 01:14:33.720
have a Canadian
passport, so it's

01:14:33.720 --> 01:14:36.750
nice to see a familiar face.

01:14:36.750 --> 01:14:40.680
I'm really interested in sort
of some nuts and bolts things.

01:14:40.680 --> 01:14:42.600
The theater kind of
traditional timeline

01:14:42.600 --> 01:14:44.820
is the tech at the end.

01:14:44.820 --> 01:14:47.890
And so many of your
pieces over the years,

01:14:47.890 --> 01:14:49.830
both in theater
and also in opera,

01:14:49.830 --> 01:14:51.810
integrate technology
so beautifully

01:14:51.810 --> 01:14:52.950
in the storytelling.

01:14:52.950 --> 01:14:54.570
I'm curious, at
what point do they

01:14:54.570 --> 01:14:57.265
begin to enter into the
process with the performers,

01:14:57.265 --> 01:14:59.640
how they respond to it, and
how important that process is

01:14:59.640 --> 01:15:00.390
to you?

01:15:00.390 --> 01:15:02.910
Well, very often,
it's from day one.

01:15:02.910 --> 01:15:05.890
I know that Brecht, at
the end of his life,

01:15:05.890 --> 01:15:08.070
Brecht was working on
this idea that everybody

01:15:08.070 --> 01:15:11.310
has to be in the
rehearsal room on day one,

01:15:11.310 --> 01:15:12.990
even if they don't
have much to do.

01:15:12.990 --> 01:15:14.070
They should be there.

01:15:14.070 --> 01:15:16.380
And, of course, that's a
very expensive process.

01:15:16.380 --> 01:15:19.320
But there's people who are
ready to do that for very cheap.

01:15:19.320 --> 01:15:21.510
And I know that,
for example, when

01:15:21.510 --> 01:15:23.820
I did the far side of the
moon, was a one man show.

01:15:23.820 --> 01:15:26.585
But the room was crowded with
people, with technicians,

01:15:26.585 --> 01:15:28.710
and all that, who didn't
have, didn't know, really,

01:15:28.710 --> 01:15:30.777
what the use of them was.

01:15:30.777 --> 01:15:32.610
But they just sit there
and they participate

01:15:32.610 --> 01:15:34.260
in the conversations
and all that.

01:15:34.260 --> 01:15:36.000
And eventually,
people do drawings.

01:15:36.000 --> 01:15:40.710
And very, very early on in the
process they suggest stuff,

01:15:40.710 --> 01:15:41.405
and you start.

01:15:41.405 --> 01:15:42.780
And certainly in
this day and age

01:15:42.780 --> 01:15:45.000
where everybody has a
PowerBook and goes on the web,

01:15:45.000 --> 01:15:48.540
and I would do an improv
and somebody, some lighting

01:15:48.540 --> 01:15:50.460
designer or technician
would say, you know,

01:15:50.460 --> 01:15:52.650
the guy you were quoting, well,
actually, the quote is this.

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:54.608
And then there's this
whole thing that happens.

01:15:54.608 --> 01:15:56.760
So now I think we
have the tools, very

01:15:56.760 --> 01:16:00.270
accessible and cheap tools,
to invite a lot of people

01:16:00.270 --> 01:16:01.560
in the rehearsal room.

01:16:01.560 --> 01:16:06.000
And not to desacralize
the rehearsal room

01:16:06.000 --> 01:16:09.660
that's usually reserved to
the director and the actor.

01:16:09.660 --> 01:16:12.570
But to, because my work
is also a writing process,

01:16:12.570 --> 01:16:14.880
I think that if everybody
there has their say,

01:16:14.880 --> 01:16:19.200
chances are their technologies
or their craft will

01:16:19.200 --> 01:16:22.890
be better integrated, will seem
more essential to the work,

01:16:22.890 --> 01:16:25.300
because it's informed
from day one.

01:16:25.300 --> 01:16:29.250
So what happens is that you
destroy all of the hierarchy.

01:16:29.250 --> 01:16:31.740
They use this
hierarchy of the writer

01:16:31.740 --> 01:16:37.020
and then the director and
then the slave actors,

01:16:37.020 --> 01:16:38.040
and then after that.

01:16:38.040 --> 01:16:40.170
And then we bring
in the technicians.

01:16:40.170 --> 01:16:44.910
And it's like that because
just for practical reasons

01:16:44.910 --> 01:16:46.090
or economical reasons.

01:16:46.090 --> 01:16:49.710
So if you could find a way to
make it current economically

01:16:49.710 --> 01:16:52.147
to have people from
day one, everybody

01:16:52.147 --> 01:16:53.730
there in the room,
and everybody knows

01:16:53.730 --> 01:16:57.270
what the project is about.

01:16:57.270 --> 01:16:58.560
I think.

01:16:58.560 --> 01:17:00.380
Of course, in opera,
that's not possible.

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:02.130
Well, it was in part
when we did the Ring.

01:17:02.130 --> 01:17:05.095
Because when we rehearsed in
[INAUDIBLE] close to Montreal--

01:17:05.095 --> 01:17:06.470
No, I'm saying
with the singers--

01:17:06.470 --> 01:17:06.810
We had a crew--

01:17:06.810 --> 01:17:07.440
The singers, the singers.

01:17:07.440 --> 01:17:10.080
We didn't have the singers, but
we did have part of the crew,

01:17:10.080 --> 01:17:13.070
and we did have a lot
of people in that room.

01:17:13.070 --> 01:17:14.070
Yes, sir.

01:17:14.070 --> 01:17:16.920
Hi, I'm David Fillingham.

01:17:16.920 --> 01:17:20.460
I graduated from Brandeis
University in 1981

01:17:20.460 --> 01:17:26.550
with a music degree, and I
went to the old Met '62 to '70.

01:17:26.550 --> 01:17:32.520
And it feels to me that a lot
of the great set designers,

01:17:32.520 --> 01:17:36.780
like Zeffirelli, et cetera,
are being very much ignored

01:17:36.780 --> 01:17:37.680
these days.

01:17:37.680 --> 01:17:39.240
I appreciate what
you do very much.

01:17:39.240 --> 01:17:41.520
I'm not in any way
suggesting otherwise.

01:17:41.520 --> 01:17:45.000
I was with Bread and Puppet
Theater from '70 to '76

01:17:45.000 --> 01:17:46.890
and we come from the same world.

01:17:46.890 --> 01:17:50.080
But it seems to me
that a lot of art

01:17:50.080 --> 01:17:54.430
is mediated through
electronics these days

01:17:54.430 --> 01:17:58.660
rather than the beauty
of the art itself.

01:17:58.660 --> 01:18:05.020
And certainly, the mechanical
brilliance of Zeffirelli

01:18:05.020 --> 01:18:08.980
and people like that
makes some of what

01:18:08.980 --> 01:18:15.400
we do look, excuse me for saying
it this way, but pedestrian.

01:18:15.400 --> 01:18:20.950
Could you talk a little
bit about art and design

01:18:20.950 --> 01:18:24.030
as opposed to technology
and design, please?

01:18:24.030 --> 01:18:24.530
Yeah.

01:18:24.530 --> 01:18:27.010
Well I would just like to say,
since you mentioned the word

01:18:27.010 --> 01:18:31.330
Zeffirelli, I feel that's
partly my territory.

01:18:31.330 --> 01:18:34.510
So, you know, Zeffirelli's
in his 80s now.

01:18:34.510 --> 01:18:39.880
And in his time, he was a
great theatrical innovator,

01:18:39.880 --> 01:18:44.290
and he did wonderful
productions.

01:18:44.290 --> 01:18:47.620
But his time was then.

01:18:47.620 --> 01:18:54.580
All art forms need to advance
through the current generations

01:18:54.580 --> 01:18:57.070
of master directors
and designers.

01:18:57.070 --> 01:18:59.570
And certainly, as far
as the Met is concerned,

01:18:59.570 --> 01:19:05.440
we do not ask for the Met to
be overrun with technology.

01:19:05.440 --> 01:19:08.890
What we ask is great
storytelling directors

01:19:08.890 --> 01:19:12.520
to create their best work
at the Met with the tools

01:19:12.520 --> 01:19:17.840
that they wish to bring in
as part of that process.

01:19:17.840 --> 01:19:22.660
So there are directors who
at the Met this past season,

01:19:22.660 --> 01:19:25.330
for example, we have
seven new productions.

01:19:25.330 --> 01:19:27.950
David McVicar, who's a very
accomplished stage director,

01:19:27.950 --> 01:19:31.510
directed a new production
of Anna Bolena, which

01:19:31.510 --> 01:19:34.070
there's nothing, there
are no projections.

01:19:34.070 --> 01:19:37.000
It's all just old
fashioned scenery,

01:19:37.000 --> 01:19:39.618
but presented with
a modern aesthetic.

01:19:39.618 --> 01:19:42.160
All the scene changes to take
place in front of the audience.

01:19:42.160 --> 01:19:45.700
We don't bring in the heavy
curtain in between each scene.

01:19:45.700 --> 01:19:49.690
You know, it's really what
makes theater and opera

01:19:49.690 --> 01:19:54.340
interesting today is the
wealth of imagination that

01:19:54.340 --> 01:20:00.460
comes from different directors
who work in creating new work.

01:20:00.460 --> 01:20:03.610
So everything has
to move forward.

01:20:03.610 --> 01:20:07.480
What Zeffirelli did in the '70s
and '60s was terrific then,

01:20:07.480 --> 01:20:08.890
but it's not so terrific now.

01:20:08.890 --> 01:20:11.170
You know, it's not, what's
not interesting, I think,

01:20:11.170 --> 01:20:15.490
for an audience today is to
see every inch of the Met

01:20:15.490 --> 01:20:18.910
proscenium stuffed with scenery.

01:20:18.910 --> 01:20:21.100
Sometimes in some of
these older productions,

01:20:21.100 --> 01:20:22.630
the scenery may
look interesting,

01:20:22.630 --> 01:20:24.730
but you have no
idea who is singing.

01:20:24.730 --> 01:20:27.160
So what we're trying
to do is bring

01:20:27.160 --> 01:20:33.310
in directors who are trying
who are in touch with

01:20:33.310 --> 01:20:37.570
and who are creating the
sensibilities and sensitivities

01:20:37.570 --> 01:20:39.130
and leading audiences today.

01:20:39.130 --> 01:20:43.480
And ultimately, with the
goal of pleasing them.

01:20:43.480 --> 01:20:46.250
Well, something,
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:20:46.250 --> 01:20:48.790
[INTERPOSING VOICES] if I
could, just that in the '60s,

01:20:48.790 --> 01:20:53.020
we had no confusion about who
was singing and what the sets

01:20:53.020 --> 01:20:54.550
were.

01:20:54.550 --> 01:20:55.475
Well.

01:20:55.475 --> 01:20:56.975
Well, something,
though, if you come

01:20:56.975 --> 01:21:00.040
to see the Tempest, for example,
that we're producing this year,

01:21:00.040 --> 01:21:03.860
it is without very, very,
very, very little technology

01:21:03.860 --> 01:21:04.360
involved.

01:21:04.360 --> 01:21:06.460
And we never have this kind of
conversation about technology

01:21:06.460 --> 01:21:07.085
on the Tempest.

01:21:07.085 --> 01:21:09.910
It's actually more of an homage
to Zeffirelli in a certain way,

01:21:09.910 --> 01:21:10.630
where you would happen.

01:21:10.630 --> 01:21:11.755
It's a question of context.

01:21:11.755 --> 01:21:16.060
Certain pieces, or certain
artistic adventures,

01:21:16.060 --> 01:21:19.940
do sometimes ask for
a new vocabulary.

01:21:19.940 --> 01:21:23.140
So that sometimes
involves technology.

01:21:23.140 --> 01:21:26.170
And of course,
because we are heavily

01:21:26.170 --> 01:21:29.893
using excerpts of contexts
where we did experiment a lot

01:21:29.893 --> 01:21:30.560
with technology.

01:21:30.560 --> 01:21:32.600
So of course, that's
part of our conversation.

01:21:32.600 --> 01:21:35.560
But there's many, many,
many, many other contexts

01:21:35.560 --> 01:21:38.080
and projects that we have that
have nothing to do with that.

01:21:38.080 --> 01:21:41.800
And where we do have
an appreciation of,

01:21:41.800 --> 01:21:45.910
and even we even do an
homage to the craft of people

01:21:45.910 --> 01:21:51.550
like Zeffirelli or early stage
designers who were amazing,

01:21:51.550 --> 01:21:53.663
amazing painters,
amazing image makers.

01:21:53.663 --> 01:21:56.080
But I think it's important
that directors have the ability

01:21:56.080 --> 01:22:00.490
to draw upon what is available
today to pursue their craft.

01:22:00.490 --> 01:22:03.850
You know, movies
today obviously don't

01:22:03.850 --> 01:22:07.060
look like they did 30
years ago, 40 years ago.

01:22:07.060 --> 01:22:10.930
When Steven Spielberg started
harnessing special effects

01:22:10.930 --> 01:22:16.150
on behalf of ET or some of the
wonderful films that he made,

01:22:16.150 --> 01:22:18.880
those films couldn't
have been possible

01:22:18.880 --> 01:22:20.245
10 or 20 years earlier.

01:22:20.245 --> 01:22:22.120
But the result, there's
a whole debate, also,

01:22:22.120 --> 01:22:24.190
in the black and white,
the amazing black

01:22:24.190 --> 01:22:25.578
and white days of movies.

01:22:25.578 --> 01:22:26.620
It was just so fantastic.

01:22:26.620 --> 01:22:29.740
And how people, even Cocteau
swore that he'd never

01:22:29.740 --> 01:22:31.510
do a color movie.

01:22:31.510 --> 01:22:33.430
Because when color
invaded, said,

01:22:33.430 --> 01:22:35.715
well, you know, then it's
all about something else.

01:22:35.715 --> 01:22:36.340
Not about real.

01:22:36.340 --> 01:22:38.650
And then he eventually gave
in at the end of his life

01:22:38.650 --> 01:22:40.510
and did a color movie.

01:22:40.510 --> 01:22:43.990
It's always, I think,
when new tools come

01:22:43.990 --> 01:22:47.860
in and new paintbrushes come
in and new colors come in,

01:22:47.860 --> 01:22:50.260
we're always wary of them
and always afraid of them,

01:22:50.260 --> 01:22:53.940
because we don't know them,
and fear is always the cause--

01:22:53.940 --> 01:22:55.240
it always comes from ignorance.

01:22:55.240 --> 01:22:56.990
And because we don't
know about something,

01:22:56.990 --> 01:22:58.960
we fear it, in a way.

01:22:58.960 --> 01:23:02.260
But then these
things kind of get

01:23:02.260 --> 01:23:06.010
introduced into the whole
array of possibilities.

01:23:06.010 --> 01:23:09.395
And so when you happen
to be at the cutting

01:23:09.395 --> 01:23:11.020
edge of these things,
of course, you're

01:23:11.020 --> 01:23:13.030
more vulnerable
to the criticism.

01:23:13.030 --> 01:23:16.120
And then if you're
using technologies

01:23:16.120 --> 01:23:19.670
that are kind of accepted
or taken for granted.

01:23:19.670 --> 01:23:21.580
[INAUDIBLE] So we
actually have time

01:23:21.580 --> 01:23:23.710
for one more question,
with apology,

01:23:23.710 --> 01:23:26.060
but our guests have
other obligations.

01:23:26.060 --> 01:23:27.910
So please, if you would.

01:23:27.910 --> 01:23:28.920
Hi, my name is Paulina.

01:23:28.920 --> 01:23:31.270
I'm a mechanical
engineer here, and I

01:23:31.270 --> 01:23:34.240
went to see Gotterdammerung
with the MIT art scholars,

01:23:34.240 --> 01:23:35.380
and it was really magical.

01:23:35.380 --> 01:23:37.463
And I think you already
answered my question, so I

01:23:37.463 --> 01:23:38.770
might let somebody else go.

01:23:38.770 --> 01:23:41.590
But I wanted to ask
whether you said

01:23:41.590 --> 01:23:43.930
that people will become
accustomed to the technology

01:23:43.930 --> 01:23:44.910
and used to seeing it.

01:23:44.910 --> 01:23:47.243
So whether you thought that
would detract from the magic

01:23:47.243 --> 01:23:48.490
or detract from the piece.

01:23:48.490 --> 01:23:52.480
No, I think that it's a question
of when Eisenstein first

01:23:52.480 --> 01:23:56.920
presented the Cuirasse
Potemkin, actually,

01:23:56.920 --> 01:23:59.800
there's people who recount
that in that first projection

01:23:59.800 --> 01:24:01.235
some people hid
behind their seats

01:24:01.235 --> 01:24:02.860
because they thought
the boat was going

01:24:02.860 --> 01:24:06.190
to come through the
screen and were distracted

01:24:06.190 --> 01:24:07.990
by a lot of these things.

01:24:07.990 --> 01:24:15.820
And the first time footlights,
electrical footlights, people

01:24:15.820 --> 01:24:18.100
actually wrote about how
electricity should not

01:24:18.100 --> 01:24:21.910
be permitted in a theater
because the organic essence

01:24:21.910 --> 01:24:23.630
of the candles and all of that.

01:24:23.630 --> 01:24:27.970
And if you see too
much the actors,

01:24:27.970 --> 01:24:29.590
you won't listen
to them as much.

01:24:29.590 --> 01:24:32.565
And what's important
in theater is the text.

01:24:32.565 --> 01:24:34.990
And all these things
were, all these comments.

01:24:34.990 --> 01:24:38.350
And eventually, people see
the advantage of seeing

01:24:38.350 --> 01:24:40.012
the actors better, you know?

01:24:40.012 --> 01:24:40.970
And that's what I mean.

01:24:40.970 --> 01:24:43.570
So that's why I'm
not, I'm not offended.

01:24:43.570 --> 01:24:46.420
And I agree that there's a
lot of dangers and flaws,

01:24:46.420 --> 01:24:48.400
and we might have
failed at times.

01:24:48.400 --> 01:24:50.700
And maybe in the
case of the Ring,

01:24:50.700 --> 01:24:52.600
it was a bit
overwhelming or whatever.

01:24:52.600 --> 01:24:54.520
But still, there are
some magical moments

01:24:54.520 --> 01:24:57.700
where a more accustomed audience
to these kind of devices

01:24:57.700 --> 01:25:00.047
were actually looking at
listening to the singer

01:25:00.047 --> 01:25:01.630
while others were
going, what is this?

01:25:01.630 --> 01:25:03.190
I've never seen this.

01:25:03.190 --> 01:25:04.300
So of course, you know.

01:25:04.300 --> 01:25:06.670
And that's fine, and that's
part of the whole process.

01:25:06.670 --> 01:25:08.870
And that's why I'm
saying, you know, in 2,

01:25:08.870 --> 01:25:14.793
3, 10 years, that'll be part
of what people are used to.

01:25:14.793 --> 01:25:16.210
And that's also
something, I think

01:25:16.210 --> 01:25:17.335
it's an important question.

01:25:17.335 --> 01:25:20.170
Because it has to
do with knowing what

01:25:20.170 --> 01:25:23.440
the audience knows
about storytelling.

01:25:23.440 --> 01:25:27.940
And 25, 30 years
ago, the audiences

01:25:27.940 --> 01:25:33.070
didn't know much about jump
cuts and flash forwards and all

01:25:33.070 --> 01:25:34.990
of this vocabulary that
television and rock

01:25:34.990 --> 01:25:38.500
videos and whatever movies
that people have seen.

01:25:38.500 --> 01:25:41.980
Now people have a vocabulary,
they're very educated people.

01:25:41.980 --> 01:25:43.990
Even people who've
never been to school

01:25:43.990 --> 01:25:46.750
go, listen to stories, go
to movies and all that.

01:25:46.750 --> 01:25:49.660
And there's this whole thing
that's developed with time.

01:25:49.660 --> 01:25:52.060
And you have to take that
into account when you

01:25:52.060 --> 01:25:53.440
try to convey a story today.

01:25:53.440 --> 01:25:54.970
If you don't do
that, and you try

01:25:54.970 --> 01:25:58.000
to respect the tradition
and all of that,

01:25:58.000 --> 01:26:00.580
people are at the end of
the play before you are.

01:26:00.580 --> 01:26:06.050
So you really have to try to
understand where people are,

01:26:06.050 --> 01:26:08.470
and how they
understand the story,

01:26:08.470 --> 01:26:12.340
and if they're ready to follow
you in any new way of saying

01:26:12.340 --> 01:26:14.140
things.

01:26:14.140 --> 01:26:18.130
Robert, Peter, just--
you've raised our sights,

01:26:18.130 --> 01:26:19.360
you've raised our spirits.

01:26:19.360 --> 01:26:20.950
You've done just,
I think, what we

01:26:20.950 --> 01:26:25.810
hoped would happen with Robert's
residency here in celebrating

01:26:25.810 --> 01:26:26.830
the McDermott Award.

01:26:26.830 --> 01:26:28.780
And so we congratulate you.

01:26:28.780 --> 01:26:29.860
MIT thanks you.

01:26:29.860 --> 01:26:32.590
I hope you'll come back, and
Peter, you're always welcome

01:26:32.590 --> 01:26:33.460
back too.

01:26:33.460 --> 01:26:34.630
So thank you, everyone.

01:26:34.630 --> 01:26:37.730
[APPLAUSE]

01:26:37.730 --> 01:26:38.230
Well done.

01:26:38.230 --> 01:26:41.280
[INAUDIBLE] Well done.

